LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t1",title:"Test 1",topics:"Coffee · Teenage sleep · Volunteering · A teenage repairer · Swimming at forty · Learning online",parts:{1:{title:"Coffee's long journey",text:`Few drinks have {0} as much influence on daily life as coffee. According to a popular legend, its effects were first {1} by a goat herder in Ethiopia, who noticed that his animals became unusually lively after eating the berries of a certain plant.

Whether or not the story is true, by the fifteenth century coffee was being grown in Yemen, and it soon {2} popular across the Middle East. Coffee houses became important meeting places where people would {3} the news, listen to music and play games.

When the drink reached Europe in the seventeenth century, not everyone was pleased. Some people {4} it of causing laziness, and a few rulers even tried to {5} it altogether. Their efforts, however, came to nothing. Coffee houses continued to {6} up in cities such as London and Vienna, and many became centres of business.

Today, around two billion cups are drunk every day, and for millions of farmers, growing coffee is their main {7} of income. It seems the goats may have {8} across something truly remarkable.`,example:{o:["had","made","done","given"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["discovered","invented","created","explored"],a:0,x:"You discover something that already exists (the effects of a plant). You invent or create something new; you explore a place or an idea."},{o:["got","became","turned","came"],a:1,x:"become + adjective for a change of state. ‘Got popular’ is too informal, ‘turned’ is used with colours or ages (turned red, turned forty), and ‘came popular’ isn't English."},{o:["argue","speak","discuss","talk"],a:2,x:"discuss is followed directly by an object: discuss the news. argue about, speak about and talk about all need a preposition."},{o:["blamed","accused","charged","criticised"],a:1,x:"accuse somebody OF doing something. Compare: blame somebody FOR, charge somebody WITH, criticise somebody FOR."},{o:["refuse","reject","deny","ban"],a:3,x:"Rulers ban something = make it illegal. refuse/reject = say no to an offer; deny = say something isn't true."},{o:["pop","come","set","grow"],a:0,x:"pop up = appear suddenly and in large numbers. ‘Come up’ means be mentioned; ‘set up’ needs an object (set up a café); ‘grow up’ is for people."},{o:["origin","source","root","base"],a:1,x:"Fixed collocation: a source of income."},{o:["put","got","found","come"],a:3,x:"come across = find something by chance. The other verbs don't form a phrasal verb with ‘across’ with this meaning."}]},2:{title:"Why teenagers are always tired",text:`Parents often complain that their teenage children stay up late and then find {0} impossible to get out of bed in the morning. It is easy to put this down to laziness, {1} scientists now believe the real explanation is biological.

During adolescence, the body starts producing the sleep hormone melatonin {2} later in the evening than it does in younger children or adults. {3} a result, many teenagers simply do not feel tired until eleven o'clock or even midnight. Since most still have to get up early for school, {4} of them get anything like enough sleep during the week.

This matters because sleep plays a vital role {5} learning and memory. Students who regularly get less {6} eight hours tend to find it harder to concentrate in class. Some schools have responded {7} starting lessons later, and early results suggest that attendance and grades have both improved. Not everyone is convinced, {8}. Some critics argue that teenagers would simply stay up even later.`,example:{a:"it"},gaps:[{a:["but","yet","although","though"],x:"A contrast between two clauses: easy to blame laziness, BUT scientists disagree."},{a:["much","far"],x:"much/far + comparative: much later. ‘A lot later’ is correct English, but it's two words, so it can't fill a one-word gap."},{a:["as"],x:"Fixed linker: as a result."},{a:["few"],x:"few of them = not many of them. ‘Little’ is for uncountable nouns; ‘none’ would need ‘gets’ and changes the meaning."},{a:["in"],x:"play a role IN something."},{a:["than"],x:"Comparative: less THAN eight hours."},{a:["by"],x:"respond BY + -ing = the way you react."},{a:["though","however"],x:"Sentence-final contrast: Not everyone is convinced, though / however."}]},3:{title:"Volunteering abroad",text:`Every year, thousands of young people spend their summer {0} abroad.
For many, the experience is a chance to do something {1} for others,
while also gaining skills that will look {2} on a CV.

However, not all projects are equally {3}. Some charge high fees but offer little
{4} to the young people who take part, and a few have even been
{5} of doing more harm than good to the communities they claim to help.

Before signing up, it is {6} to find out exactly how your money will be spent
and whether the project has the full {7} of the local community.
Choosing {8}, experts say, is the key to a rewarding trip.`,example:{w:"VOLUNTEER",a:"volunteering"},gaps:[{w:"USE",a:["useful"],x:"Adjective after ‘something’: something useful."},{w:"IMPRESS",a:["impressive"],x:"look + adjective: look impressive on a CV."},{w:"BENEFIT",a:["beneficial"],x:"Adjective after ‘equally’: equally beneficial."},{w:"GUIDE",a:["guidance"],x:"Uncountable noun after ‘little’: offer little guidance."},{w:"SUSPECT",a:["suspected"],x:"Passive: have been suspected of doing something."},{w:"ADVISE",a:["advisable"],x:"it is + adjective + to-infinitive: it is advisable to…"},{w:"APPROVE",a:["approval"],x:"Noun after ‘the full’: have the full approval of."},{w:"CARE",a:["carefully"],x:"Adverb describing how you choose: choosing carefully."}]},4:{example:{s1:"A very friendly taxi driver drove us into town.",key:"DRIVEN",pre:"We",post:"a very friendly taxi driver.",ans:"were driven into town by"},items:[{s1:"I haven't been to the cinema for over a year.",key:"LAST",pre:"It's over a year",post:"the cinema.",a:[["since i last"],["went to"]],x:"It's + time + since + subject + last + past simple."},{s1:"‘Don't touch the paint – it's still wet,’ the decorator told us.",key:"WARNED",pre:"The decorator",post:"the paint because it was still wet.",a:[["warned us"],["not to touch"]],x:"warn somebody (not) to do something. The object (us) is needed."},{s1:"It's possible that Jack forgot about the meeting.",key:"MIGHT",pre:"Jack",post:"about the meeting.",a:[["might have"],["forgotten"]],x:"Past possibility: might have + past participle."},{s1:"I regret not studying harder for my exams.",key:"WISH",pre:"I",post:"harder for my exams.",a:[["wish i had","wish i'd"],["studied"]],x:"Regret about the past: wish + past perfect."},{s1:"The concert was cancelled because the singer was ill.",key:"DUE",pre:"The concert was cancelled",post:"illness.",a:[["due to"],["the singer's"]],x:"due to + noun phrase. The adjective ‘ill’ becomes the noun ‘illness’, so you need the possessive: the singer's illness."},{s1:"Nobody in the class is taller than Sam.",key:"TALLEST",pre:"Sam",post:"the class.",a:[["is the tallest"],["person in","student in","one in","pupil in","boy in","girl in","in"]],x:"Nobody is taller than X = X is the tallest (person) in… Remember ‘in the class’, not ‘of the class’."}]},5:{title:"The boy who fixes phones",intro:"an article about a teenager who set up a repair business",paras:["When Tomás Reyes was fourteen, his mother's phone slipped out of her hand at a bus stop and landed screen-down on the pavement. The repair shop in town quoted a price that was almost as much as a new phone, so Tomás, who had always enjoyed taking things apart, decided to have a go himself. He watched a dozen online videos, ordered a replacement screen for a fraction of the price and spent an entire weekend at the kitchen table. ‘I broke two tiny cables and nearly gave up,’ he remembers. ‘But when it finally switched on, I felt like I'd performed surgery.’","Word spread quickly. Within a month, friends and neighbours were turning up at the door with cracked screens and dead batteries. At first Tomás refused to charge anything, partly because he wasn't confident that the repairs would last. It was his grandfather, a retired electrician, who persuaded him otherwise. ‘He told me that if I worked for free, people would never take me seriously – and neither would I.’","Now seventeen, Tomás runs a small business from a converted garage, fixing around thirty devices a week after school and on Saturdays. He keeps his prices low and offers a six-month guarantee on every job, something he admits was a risky decision. ‘If lots of repairs had failed, I'd have lost a fortune. But it forced me to be really careful, and it means customers trust me.’","Running a business while studying for exams has not been easy. His teachers were initially concerned that his grades would suffer, and for a while they did. The turning point came when he started treating schoolwork in the same way as his repairs: breaking big tasks into small, manageable steps and doing one at a time. ‘Revising for a history exam isn't that different from replacing a charging port,’ he laughs. ‘If you rush, you make a mess.’","Tomás is also passionate about the environmental side of his work. Millions of phones are thrown away every year, many of them with faults that could be repaired in less than an hour. He has started running free workshops at the local library, where he shows people how to do simple repairs themselves. Some of his customers find this puzzling. ‘One man asked me why I was teaching people to do my job,’ he says. ‘I told him there are more broken phones in the world than I could fix in a hundred lifetimes.’","As for the future, Tomás hopes to study engineering at university, although he has no intention of closing the garage. ‘Maybe one day I'll design phones that are easier to fix,’ he says. ‘That would put me out of business – but I think I could live with that.’"],qs:[{q:"How did Tomás feel when he finished repairing his mother's phone?",o:["relieved that he hadn't caused more damage","proud of what he had achieved","surprised at how simple the job had been","annoyed that it had taken so long"],a:1,x:"‘I felt like I'd performed surgery’ shows pride. He nearly gave up, so the job wasn't simple (C), and he doesn't mention relief or annoyance."},{q:"Why did Tomás's grandfather advise him to charge for repairs?",o:["so that he could afford better equipment","because his repairs were already of a high quality","so that he would value his own work","because customers were taking advantage of him"],a:2,x:"‘People would never take me seriously – and neither would I.’ Charging would make Tomás respect his own work too."},{q:"What does ‘something’ refer to in paragraph 3?",o:["keeping his prices low","working from a garage","fixing thirty devices a week","offering a guarantee on repairs"],a:3,x:"‘…offers a six-month guarantee on every job, something he admits was a risky decision.’ He then explains the risk: failed repairs would cost him a fortune."},{q:"What helped Tomás to improve his school results?",o:["applying a method he uses in his work","reducing the number of repairs he did","getting extra support from his teachers","focusing on the subjects he enjoys"],a:0,x:"He started ‘treating schoolwork in the same way as his repairs’ – small steps, one at a time."},{q:"Why does Tomás run free workshops?",o:["to attract new customers to his business","because the library asked him for help","to find people who could work for him","because he wants fewer phones to be thrown away"],a:3,x:"The paragraph is about ‘the environmental side of his work’ – millions of repairable phones are thrown away."},{q:"What does Tomás suggest in the final paragraph?",o:["He plans to close his business when he goes to university.","He wouldn't mind if his business was no longer needed.","He is worried about competition from phone designers.","He has already started designing his own phones."],a:1,x:"‘That would put me out of business – but I think I could live with that.’ He has no intention of closing the garage (A)."}]},6:{title:"Learning to swim at forty",intro:"an article about a woman who learned to swim as an adult",paras:["Until last year, Rachel Dunn had a secret that she had kept from almost everyone, including some of her closest friends: she couldn't swim. As a child she had been frightened of water, and by the time she was an adult, the embarrassment had become worse than the fear. {1} ‘I said I didn't like getting my hair wet, or that I'd forgotten my swimming things,’ she says.","Everything changed when her daughter, then eight, asked her to come into the pool during a birthday party. Rachel stayed on the side, watching the other parents splash about with their children. {2} That evening, she searched online for adult swimming lessons and booked the first available class.","The first lesson was harder than she had expected. The group met at seven in the morning, and the water felt freezing. The instructor, a patient former competitive swimmer called Mo, didn't ask anyone to swim at all. {3} ‘We just stood in the shallow end and practised breathing out under water,’ Rachel explains. ‘It sounds silly, but I was shaking.’","Over the following weeks, progress was slow but steady. Rachel learned to float, then to kick, and finally to move her arms and legs at the same time. There were setbacks, too. {4} It took her a fortnight to find the courage to go back.","What surprised her most was how much the other learners helped. The group included a retired lorry driver, a university student and a nurse who had grown up hundreds of miles from the sea. {5} ‘Nobody was judging anybody. We were all equally bad, which was a great comfort.’","Last month, Rachel swam her first full length of the pool without stopping. Her daughter was there to cheer her on. {6} ‘Next summer we're going to the coast,’ she says, ‘and this time I won't be sitting on the beach making excuses.’"],options:["On one occasion she swallowed a mouthful of water, panicked and climbed straight out of the pool.","It was, Rachel says, one of the proudest moments of her life.","Whenever friends suggested a trip to the beach or the pool, she invented a reason not to go.","Many of them had been taught by the same instructor as children.","Instead, he spent most of the session helping the group get used to having their faces in the water.","Despite their very different backgrounds, they shared the same determination to overcome their fears.","She remembers feeling that she was missing out on something important."],key:[2,6,4,0,5,1],x:["C: ‘invented a reason not to go’ is followed by examples of her excuses (‘I said I didn't like getting my hair wet…’).","G: watching other parents play makes her feel she is missing out, which explains why she booked lessons ‘that evening’.","E: ‘Instead’ contrasts with ‘didn't ask anyone to swim’, and ‘having their faces in the water’ links to ‘breathing out under water’.","A: the setback (panicking and climbing out) explains why ‘it took her a fortnight to find the courage to go back’.","F: ‘very different backgrounds’ refers to the lorry driver, student and nurse. D is wrong – the learners are adults who never learned to swim.","B: her first full length with her daughter watching is ‘one of the proudest moments of her life’."]},7:{title:"Learning online",intro:"an article in which four people describe learning a new skill online",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Priya",text:["I started learning Japanese on a free app during a long winter when I had very little else to do. At first I treated it like a game, and I was hooked on keeping my daily streak going. The trouble was that after six months I could recognise hundreds of words but couldn't hold even the simplest conversation. So I found an online tutor in Osaka, and we now talk twice a week. Those sessions were terrifying at first – I kept apologising for my mistakes – but she's incredibly encouraging. I've also discovered that I learn far more when I have a real reason to use the language, so I've started messaging a group of Japanese students who want to practise their English."]},{name:"Marcus",text:["I've wanted to play the guitar since I was a teenager, but I always assumed I'd need expensive lessons. Then a friend lent me an old guitar and pointed me towards some online video courses. What I like is that I can watch a difficult section as many times as I need without anyone watching me struggle. On the other hand, there's nobody to tell you when you're doing something wrong, and I developed some bad habits that took ages to correct. My fingertips were really sore for the first few weeks, too, which nearly put me off completely. Now, two years on, I play in a small band with some colleagues from the office. We're not brilliant, but we have a lot of fun."]},{name:"Hannah",text:["I work in a bookshop, and I decided to learn to code mainly because I was curious about how websites work. I chose a course with a clear structure, weekly deadlines and graded projects, because I know myself: without someone setting targets, I'd give up. The course had an online forum where students helped each other, and honestly, that was worth more than the lessons. Whenever I got stuck, someone would reply within minutes. It was hard to fit study around full-time work, and I did consider stopping more than once. But I finished, and I've since built a new website for the bookshop, which has really increased our online sales. My manager was so impressed that she's given me a pay rise."]},{name:"Leo",text:["My grandmother was a wonderful cook, and when she died I realised that nobody in the family had ever written down her recipes. That's what got me interested in learning to cook properly. I've mostly used short online videos, which are great for learning individual techniques, like how to chop an onion quickly. What they don't teach you is how to plan a whole meal, and my early dinner parties were chaotic – everything was ready at different times. I've recently joined a live online class where a chef cooks along with us and I can ask questions as I go. My goal is to recreate my grandmother's dishes. I haven't got her lemon cake right yet, but I'm getting closer."]}],qs:[{q:"chose a course because they need deadlines to stay motivated?",a:2,x:"Hannah: ‘without someone setting targets, I'd give up’."},{q:"mentions a physical difficulty they had at the beginning?",a:1,x:"Marcus: ‘My fingertips were really sore for the first few weeks’."},{q:"explains that a family loss led them to start learning?",a:3,x:"Leo: ‘when she died I realised that nobody… had written down her recipes’."},{q:"felt anxious about making errors in front of another person?",a:0,x:"Priya: the sessions were ‘terrifying’ and she ‘kept apologising for my mistakes’."},{q:"found other learners more helpful than the course materials?",a:2,x:"Hannah: the forum ‘was worth more than the lessons’."},{q:"says the resources they used didn't cover an important part of the skill?",a:3,x:"Leo: videos don't teach ‘how to plan a whole meal’."},{q:"appreciates being able to repeat parts of a lesson privately?",a:1,x:"Marcus: he can watch a section many times ‘without anyone watching me struggle’."},{q:"realised that their early progress had not been practical?",a:0,x:"Priya: she knew hundreds of words ‘but couldn't hold even the simplest conversation’."},{q:"now uses the skill socially with people from work?",a:1,x:"Marcus plays in a band ‘with some colleagues from the office’."},{q:"has been rewarded at work for using their new skill?",a:2,x:"Hannah built the shop's website and got ‘a pay rise’."}]}}}),LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t2",title:"Test 2",topics:"Four-day week · Escape rooms · Lists · A job interview · City birds · Working abroad",parts:{1:{title:"The four-day week",text:`When a software company in New Zealand {0} a four-day week for its staff, with no reduction in pay, many business experts expected productivity to {1}. Instead, the opposite happened.

Employees reported feeling less stressed and more {2} about coming to work. Because they knew they had one day less to complete their tasks, they wasted less time in long meetings and {3} more carefully on the work that really mattered.

Hundreds of companies around the world have since {4} the idea a try. Not all of them have seen such impressive results, however. In some industries, such as healthcare, it is difficult to reduce working hours without {5} the quality of care. Critics also {6} out that a shorter week can simply mean longer, more tiring days.

Even so, surveys suggest that most workers would {7} for a four-day week if they were given the choice, and many say they would even {8} a lower salary in exchange for more free time.`,example:{o:["introduced","presented","produced","performed"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["lower","reduce","fall","cut"],a:2,x:"fall is intransitive (productivity falls). lower, reduce and cut all need an object (reduce costs)."},{o:["eager","keen","fond","enthusiastic"],a:3,x:"enthusiastic ABOUT something. Compare: keen ON, fond OF, eager TO do / FOR something."},{o:["paid","concentrated","gave","put"],a:1,x:"concentrate ON something. ‘Paid attention to’ would need the noun ‘attention’."},{o:["made","done","given","had"],a:2,x:"Fixed phrase: give something a try."},{o:["breaking","wounding","injuring","damaging"],a:3,x:"damage the quality of something. wound and injure are used for bodies; break for objects or rules."},{o:["point","make","bring","set"],a:0,x:"point out that = mention a fact, often one people have forgotten."},{o:["choose","decide","opt","prefer"],a:2,x:"opt FOR something. choose and prefer take a direct object; decide takes ON."},{o:["agree","accept","admit","approve"],a:1,x:"accept a lower salary = agree to take it. agree needs TO, approve needs OF, admit means confess."}]},2:{title:"The rise of the escape room",text:`Escape rooms, {0} which groups of players are locked in a room and must solve puzzles to get out, have become one of the fastest-growing forms of entertainment. The first ones appeared in Japan in 2007, and there are now thousands {1} over the world.

The idea is simple. Players have {2} more than an hour to find hidden clues, crack codes and open locks. {3} they fail, a member of staff lets them out anyway, so nobody is actually trapped!

Part of the appeal is that escape rooms reward teamwork rather {4} individual skill. No single player is likely to have all the knowledge needed, so success depends {5} how well the group communicates. For this reason, many companies now use them for team-building days.

Designers are constantly coming {6} with new themes, from ancient temples to space stations. Some rooms are so popular that you have to book weeks in advance, and {7} the hardest ones, fewer than one in five teams manage to escape {8} time.`,example:{a:"in"},gaps:[{a:["all"],x:"all over the world = everywhere in the world."},{a:["no"],x:"no more than = only, a maximum of."},{a:["if"],x:"Conditional: IF they fail, … (‘When’ is possible in speech but the key accepts IF.)"},{a:["than"],x:"rather THAN."},{a:["on","upon"],x:"depend ON something."},{a:["up"],x:"come up with = think of an idea."},{a:["in","for","with"],x:"in / for / with the hardest ones = in the case of the hardest rooms."},{a:["in"],x:"in time = before the time runs out. (‘On time’ means punctually, not at the last moment.)"}]},3:{title:"Why we love lists",text:`Lists are everywhere, from shopping lists to online articles with titles like
‘Ten {0} places to visit before you die’. Psychologists say there is a simple
{1} for their popularity. Our brains find information much easier to process
when it is {2} into clear sections. A list also gives readers a sense of
{3}: they know exactly how much is left to read.

For people who suffer from {4}, writing a to-do list can be particularly helpful,
and crossing items off gives a feeling of {5} that helps to reduce stress.

However, lists can also be {6}. Many online ‘top ten’ articles are
{7} chosen and based on very little real research,
so it is wise to read them {8}.`,example:{w:"AMAZE",a:"amazing"},gaps:[{w:"EXPLAIN",a:["explanation"],x:"Noun after ‘a simple’: a simple explanation FOR something."},{w:"ORGANISE",a:["organised","organized"],x:"Passive participle: when it is organised into sections. US spelling with z is accepted."},{w:"CERTAIN",a:["certainty"],x:"Noun after ‘a sense of’."},{w:"ANXIOUS",a:["anxiety"],x:"Noun after ‘suffer from’."},{w:"ACHIEVE",a:["achievement"],x:"Noun after ‘a feeling of’."},{w:"MISLEAD",a:["misleading"],x:"Adjective after ‘be’: lists can be misleading."},{w:"RANDOM",a:["randomly"],x:"Adverb before a past participle: randomly chosen."},{w:"CRITIC",a:["critically"],x:"Adverb describing how you read: read them critically."}]},4:{items:[{s1:"‘I'll pay for the meal,’ said Daniel.",key:"INSISTED",pre:"Daniel",post:"the meal.",a:[["insisted on"],["paying for"]],x:"insist ON + -ing."},{s1:"It was so cold that we couldn't go swimming.",key:"TOO",pre:"It was",post:"to go swimming.",a:[["too cold"],["for us"]],x:"too + adjective + for somebody + to-infinitive."},{s1:"I'd prefer to stay at home tonight.",key:"RATHER",pre:"I",post:"at home tonight.",a:[["would rather","'d rather"],["stay"]],x:"would rather + bare infinitive (no ‘to’)."},{s1:"They didn't let us take photos inside the museum.",key:"ALLOWED",pre:"We",post:"photos inside the museum.",a:[["were not allowed"],["to take"]],x:"Passive of let: be allowed to do something. (weren't = were not)"},{s1:"Maria started learning the violin five years ago.",key:"BEEN",pre:"Maria",post:"the violin for five years.",a:[["has been","'s been"],["learning","playing"]],x:"Present perfect continuous + for: has been learning … for five years."},{s1:"Getting tickets for the final was very difficult for us.",key:"DIFFICULTY",pre:"We",post:"tickets for the final.",a:[["had great difficulty","had real difficulty","had difficulty"],["getting","in getting","buying","in buying"]],x:"have (great) difficulty (in) + -ing."}]},5:{title:"The interview",intro:"an extract from a novel",kind:"extract",paras:["Nadia arrived at the Fairfax Gallery twenty minutes early, which turned out to be a mistake. The receptionist, a young man with enormous glasses, looked at her as if she had come to deliver a parcel and told her to wait on a hard wooden bench beneath a painting of a very unhappy horse. From there she had an excellent view of the other candidates arriving: three of them, each more elegant than the last, each greeted warmly by name.","She took out her notes and read them again, though by now she knew them almost by heart. Her sister had helped her prepare the night before, firing questions at her across the kitchen table until nearly midnight. ‘Just be yourself,’ Laila had said at the end, which Nadia thought was easy advice to give when you already had a job you loved.","At exactly ten o'clock a door opened and a tall woman in a grey suit called her name. This, Nadia realised, must be Helen Marsh, the gallery director, whose photograph she had studied online. In person she looked far less severe. She shook Nadia's hand firmly and apologised for the bench. ‘We keep meaning to replace it,’ she said. ‘Candidates tell us it's the most uncomfortable part of the whole process.’","The interview room was small and full of light. Two other people sat behind a long table: an older man who didn't introduce himself and a woman about Nadia's age who smiled constantly. The first questions were the ones she had expected, and she answered them well, or at least without stumbling. Then the older man leaned forward. ‘Tell me,’ he said, ‘about a painting you hate.’","Nadia's mind went completely blank. None of the questions Laila had invented had been anything like this. She could feel the smiling woman watching her, and for a terrible moment she considered naming something famous and safe. Instead, she heard herself describing the unhappy horse in the corridor: its strange, stiff legs, the way its eyes seemed to follow you, the fact that someone had chosen to hang it exactly where nervous people would have to sit and stare at it.","There was a pause. Then Helen Marsh laughed – a real laugh, not a polite one – and the older man wrote something down. Nadia had no idea whether this was a good sign. It was only much later, on the train home, that she allowed herself to think that it might have been."],qs:[{q:"What do we learn about Nadia's arrival in the first paragraph?",o:["She was annoyed that the receptionist was rude to her.","She felt she was treated differently from the other candidates.","She regretted not dressing more smartly.","She was impressed by the painting in the waiting area."],a:1,x:"The receptionist looks at her like a delivery person, while the others are ‘greeted warmly by name’."},{q:"What does Nadia suggest about her sister's advice?",o:["It was easy for Laila to say in her position.","It was the most useful thing Laila told her.","It made her feel more confident.","It contradicted Laila's earlier advice."],a:0,x:"‘easy advice to give when you already had a job you loved’ – Laila isn't in Nadia's situation."},{q:"When Nadia meets Helen Marsh, she finds that the director",o:["is more formal than she had expected.","seems less strict than her photograph suggested.","doesn't remember her application.","is embarrassed about the gallery's appearance."],a:1,x:"‘In person she looked far less severe.’ She only apologises for the bench (D is too general)."},{q:"The phrase ‘or at least without stumbling’ in paragraph 4 suggests that Nadia",o:["found the first questions very difficult.","was pleased with how fluent she sounded.","hadn't prepared for the first questions.","wasn't sure her answers were impressive."],a:3,x:"She corrects ‘answered them well’ to something more modest: she didn't make mistakes, but may not have impressed."},{q:"Why did Nadia talk about the horse painting?",o:["She had planned to mention it if she got a difficult question.","She knew the interviewers disliked it too.","It was the first thing that came into her mind.","She wanted to criticise the gallery's decisions."],a:2,x:"‘she heard herself describing’ shows it wasn't planned – her mind had gone blank."},{q:"How does Nadia feel at the end of the extract?",o:["certain that she will be offered the job","disappointed by the director's reaction","uncertain at first, then cautiously hopeful","worried that she has offended the older man"],a:2,x:"She ‘had no idea whether this was a good sign’, but later ‘allowed herself to think that it might have been’."}]},6:{title:"Wild in the city",intro:"an article about a photographer who takes pictures of wild birds in a city",paras:["Most wildlife photographers travel to remote forests or distant islands in search of their subjects. James Okafor takes the bus. For the past six years, the 34-year-old has photographed wild birds in the centre of Manchester, one of Britain's busiest cities. {1} ‘People are always amazed by what lives just above their heads,’ he says.","James first picked up a camera after losing his job in an office. With time on his hands, he began going for long walks, and one day he noticed a pair of falcons nesting on the roof of a church. {2} By the end of that spring, he had spent more than two hundred hours watching them.","Photographing birds in a city brings its own challenges. Unlike in the countryside, there is constant noise and movement, and the birds are often high up on buildings. {3} Security guards have occasionally asked him what he is doing, suspicious of a man pointing a long lens at office windows.","Over time, James has learned to predict where the birds will be. Herons, for example, gather along the canal early in the morning, when there are fewer people around. {4} ‘Once you understand their routine, it's surprisingly easy to be in the right place at the right time.’","His work has also proved useful to scientists. Researchers at a local university have used his photographs to count how many birds nest in the city each year. {5} James is delighted. ‘I never imagined my hobby could be of any real value,’ he admits.","Next year, James plans to publish a book of his photographs. He hopes it will encourage more people to look up. {6} ‘You don't need expensive equipment or to travel anywhere special,’ he says. ‘You just need a bit of patience.’"],options:["There are also problems of a more human kind.","Many photographers believe that the best pictures are taken in the middle of the day.","His pictures of kestrels, herons and even peregrine falcons have attracted thousands of followers online.","Their findings suggest that some species are doing better in urban areas than in the surrounding countryside.","He went back the next morning, and the morning after that.","In his view, noticing nature is something anyone can do, wherever they live.","Falcons, on the other hand, prefer to hunt in the late afternoon, using tall buildings as lookout points."],key:[2,4,0,6,3,5],x:["C: the pictures ‘have attracted thousands of followers’, which leads to ‘People are always amazed…’.","E: ‘He went back the next morning, and the morning after that’ explains how he spent two hundred hours watching.","A: ‘problems of a more human kind’ introduces the security guards, after the natural challenges.","G: ‘on the other hand’ contrasts falcons with herons – both examples of predictable routines. B contradicts the idea of timing around the birds.","D: ‘Their findings’ refers back to the researchers, and explains why James is delighted.","F: ‘noticing nature is something anyone can do’ links to ‘look up’ and ‘You don't need expensive equipment’."]},7:{title:"A new life abroad",intro:"an article in which four people describe moving to another country for work",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Sofia",text:["I moved to Vancouver for a job in a design studio, and I'll be honest: the first three months were lonely. Everyone was friendly at work, but people went straight home afterwards, and I didn't know how to make friends outside the office. What changed things was joining a hiking club. Every weekend we'd explore a different mountain trail, and those long walks made it easy to talk. The cost of living is the one thing I still struggle with. Rent takes nearly half my salary, so I share a flat with two other people, which I never expected to be doing at twenty-nine. Still, when I see the mountains from my window in the morning, I don't regret coming for a second."]},{name:"Tom",text:["I'd been teaching English in the UK for a few years when a school in Seoul offered me a two-year contract. I said yes before I'd really thought about it. The language was the biggest shock – I'd learned a few phrases, but on my first day I couldn't even work out how to buy a train ticket. My students found this hilarious, and in a way it helped, because they started teaching me Korean words at the end of every lesson. The working culture took some getting used to as well: people stay late, and at first I felt guilty leaving on time. My contract finishes next summer, and I'm genuinely torn about whether to renew it or go home."]},{name:"Amira",text:["My company transferred me to its Rotterdam office, so practical things like finding a flat were organised for me, which made the move far less stressful than it could have been. The Dutch are very direct, and in my first meetings I thought my colleagues were being rude when they disagreed with my ideas so openly. I now realise it's simply the way things are done, and I actually find it refreshing – you always know where you stand. I cycle everywhere, which has done wonders for my fitness. The only thing I really miss is my family. We video-call every Sunday, but it isn't the same as sitting round the table together."]},{name:"Daniel",text:["I went to Melbourne on a working holiday visa, planning to stay for a year, travel a bit and come home. That was six years ago. I started out picking fruit on farms, which was exhausting but taught me a lot about hard work, and then got a job in a café. Eventually I trained as a chef, and now I manage a restaurant. My parents were worried at first that I was wasting my degree, but they've visited twice and they understand now. The distance from home is hard – a flight takes almost a whole day – but I've built a life here that I couldn't have imagined when I first arrived."]}],qs:[{q:"found that a shared activity helped them to make friends?",a:0,x:"Sofia: joining a hiking club – ‘those long walks made it easy to talk’."},{q:"at first misunderstood the way people communicate at work?",a:2,x:"Amira thought colleagues ‘were being rude’, but it's ‘simply the way things are done’."},{q:"has stayed far longer than they originally intended?",a:3,x:"Daniel planned to stay a year – ‘That was six years ago’."},{q:"accepted an offer without thinking it through?",a:1,x:"Tom: ‘I said yes before I'd really thought about it’."},{q:"benefited from help provided by their employer?",a:2,x:"Amira: ‘practical things like finding a flat were organised for me’."},{q:"mentions a living arrangement they didn't expect to have at their age?",a:0,x:"Sofia shares a flat, ‘which I never expected to be doing at twenty-nine’."},{q:"says that other people's amusement at their difficulties turned out to be useful?",a:1,x:"Tom's students found it hilarious and started teaching him Korean words."},{q:"says their family had doubts about the path they chose?",a:3,x:"Daniel's parents ‘were worried… that I was wasting my degree’."},{q:"is undecided about what to do next?",a:1,x:"Tom is ‘genuinely torn about whether to renew it or go home’."},{q:"mentions an improvement in their physical health?",a:2,x:"Amira: cycling ‘has done wonders for my fitness’."}]}}}),LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t3",title:"Test 3",topics:"Street food · Procrastination · Home robots · A month offline · Outdoor school · Unusual jobs",parts:{1:{title:"Street food goes upmarket",text:`Street food has {0} a long way in recent years. Once seen as a cheap and not always very clean {1} to restaurant meals, it is now a major part of the food scene in many cities.

In London, for example, dozens of markets have opened in car parks, old railway arches and empty warehouses. Many of the stalls are {2} by young chefs who cannot afford the high rents of a traditional restaurant. A market stall allows them to {3} out new recipes at low cost and build up a loyal {4} of customers.

Some of these chefs have gone on to open successful restaurants of their own. {5}, not everyone is happy about the trend. Local restaurant owners {6} that stalls pay far less in rent and taxes, which gives them an unfair advantage. Residents living nearby have also {7} concerns about noise and litter.

Nevertheless, street food shows no sign of disappearing. For many people, eating outdoors at a busy market is simply more fun than sitting in a formal restaurant, and it's hard to {8} with that.`,example:{o:["come","gone","moved","travelled"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["replacement","substitute","option","alternative"],a:3,x:"an alternative TO something. Compare: a substitute/replacement FOR."},{o:["run","held","done","directed"],a:0,x:"run a business / a stall = manage it. (A stall is run by someone.)"},{o:["check","make","try","give"],a:2,x:"try out = test something new to see if it works."},{o:["crowd","following","audience","team"],a:1,x:"a loyal following = a group of regular supporters."},{o:["Although","Despite","However","Whereas"],a:2,x:"A sentence adverb followed by a comma is needed: However, … Although, Despite and Whereas must join two parts of one sentence."},{o:["criticise","complain","oppose","disapprove"],a:1,x:"complain THAT + clause. criticise and oppose take an object; disapprove needs OF."},{o:["risen","arisen","lifted","raised"],a:3,x:"raise concerns = express worries. rise and arise are intransitive (they don't take an object)."},{o:["dispute","deny","argue","doubt"],a:2,x:"It's hard to argue WITH that = it's difficult to disagree. dispute, deny and doubt take a direct object."}]},2:{title:"Why we put things off",text:`Almost everyone has put off an important task at some point, {0} it's a school project or a visit to the dentist. For a long time, procrastination was seen as a problem of poor time management, but psychologists now believe it has more to do {1} our emotions.

When we are faced with a task that makes us feel anxious or bored, our brains look for a way to escape {2} unpleasant feeling. Checking social media or tidying the kitchen offers instant relief, {3} only for a short time. The task is still there, and the stress of avoiding it tends to grow.

The good news is that the habit can be broken. One of the most effective methods is to make a task seem less overwhelming {4} dividing it into smaller steps. Another is to promise yourself you will work on something for {5} five minutes. {6} started, most people find it much easier to carry on. It also helps to forgive yourself. In one study, students {7} forgave themselves for putting off revision before one exam were less likely to do {8} again before the next.`,example:{a:"whether"},gaps:[{a:["with"],x:"have to do WITH = be connected to."},{a:["the","that","this"],x:"A determiner is needed before ‘unpleasant feeling’ – the/that/this feeling."},{a:["but","though","although"],x:"Contrast: instant relief, BUT only for a short time."},{a:["by"],x:"BY + -ing shows the method: by dividing it."},{a:["just","only"],x:"just / only five minutes = no more than."},{a:["once"],x:"Once started = as soon as you have started."},{a:["who","that"],x:"Defining relative clause about people: students WHO forgave themselves."},{a:["so","it"],x:"do so / do it = repeat the action (putting off revision)."}]},3:{title:"Robots in the home",text:`Robots are becoming an {0} common sight in our homes.
{1} vacuum cleaners that move around by themselves are already popular,
and many people now rely on {2} assistants that answer questions and play music.

Engineers believe that robots will soon carry out more {3} tasks, such as folding clothes.
For older people living alone, this could make a real {4} to their quality of life:
robots could remind them to take medicine and call for help in an {5}.

However, some experts are {6} about privacy,
since these machines collect large amounts of {7} information.
Others fear that we may become too {8} on technology to look after ourselves.`,example:{w:"INCREASE",a:"increasingly"},gaps:[{w:"AUTOMATE",a:["automatic","automated"],x:"Adjective before a noun: automatic vacuum cleaners."},{w:"DIGIT",a:["digital"],x:"Adjective: digital assistants."},{w:"COMPLICATE",a:["complicated"],x:"Adjective after ‘more’: more complicated tasks."},{w:"DIFFER",a:["difference"],x:"Fixed phrase: make a difference TO something."},{w:"EMERGE",a:["emergency"],x:"Noun: in an emergency."},{w:"WORRY",a:["worried"],x:"be worried ABOUT something (a feeling, so -ed)."},{w:"PERSON",a:["personal"],x:"Adjective before ‘information’: personal information."},{w:"DEPEND",a:["dependent","dependant"],x:"be dependent ON something."}]},4:{items:[{s1:"Although it was raining, we went for a walk.",key:"SPITE",pre:"",post:"we went for a walk.",a:[["in spite of"],["the rain","it raining"]],x:"in spite of + noun or -ing: In spite of the rain, …"},{s1:"The last time I saw Emma was at her wedding.",key:"SEEN",pre:"I",post:"her wedding.",a:[["have not seen"],["emma since","her since"]],x:"The last time I saw… = I haven't seen… since…"},{s1:"Lucy is sorry that she didn't go to the party.",key:"WISHES",pre:"Lucy",post:"to the party.",a:[["wishes she had","wishes she'd"],["gone","been"]],x:"Regret about the past: wish + past perfect."},{s1:"Could you open the window, please?",key:"MIND",pre:"Would",post:"the window, please?",a:[["you mind"],["opening"]],x:"Would you mind + -ing?"},{s1:"The film was so boring that I fell asleep.",key:"SUCH",pre:"It was",post:"that I fell asleep.",a:[["such a"],["boring film"]],x:"such + a + adjective + noun + that."},{s1:"People say that the castle is haunted.",key:"SAID",pre:"The castle",post:"haunted.",a:[["is said"],["to be"]],x:"Impersonal passive: subject + is said + to-infinitive."}]},5:{title:"A month offline",intro:"an article by a journalist who spent a month without a smartphone",paras:["I first realised I had a problem on a Tuesday evening in March. I'd sat down to watch a film with my partner, and when it finished, I couldn't have told you a single thing that had happened in it. I had spent the entire two hours scrolling through my phone – checking emails, reading news I'd already read, looking at photos of people I hadn't spoken to in years. That was when I decided to try an experiment: one month without a smartphone.","The rules were simple. I would swap my phone for a basic model that could only make calls and send texts. I'd still use a laptop for work, but only during office hours. My editor thought it was a brilliant idea for an article; my friends were less enthusiastic, mainly because they would now have to phone me to arrange anything. ‘Nobody phones anyone any more,’ one of them complained, as if I'd suggested communicating by carrier pigeon.","The first week was genuinely uncomfortable. I kept reaching into my pocket for a phone that wasn't there, and without a map app I got lost twice on the way to meetings, in parts of the city I thought I knew well. Waiting for a bus felt endless. I noticed, with some embarrassment, that I had completely lost the ability to simply stand still and do nothing.","By the second week, though, something had shifted. I started reading on the train again – actual books, which I had once loved and had somehow stopped buying. I had long conversations with the man who runs the newsagent's near my flat, whom I'd been saying a distracted ‘thanks’ to for five years. I also slept better than I had in months, although I can't be sure whether that was due to the experiment or to the fact that I'd started going to bed earlier out of boredom.","Not everything improved. Organising a surprise party for my sister was a nightmare, since the rest of the family planned it all in a group chat I couldn't see. And I missed being able to take photos: there were several moments, like a spectacular sunset over the river, that I wished I could have kept.","When the month ended, I did go back to my smartphone, but not in the same way. I've deleted most social media apps, and the phone now stays in a drawer in the evenings. I'm not claiming to have found the answer. But I have, at least, started watching films again – and remembering what happens in them."],qs:[{q:"What made the writer decide to try the experiment?",o:["Her partner complained about how often she used her phone.","She realised she hadn't paid attention to something she was meant to be enjoying.","Her editor asked her to write about phone addiction.","She was worried about losing contact with old friends."],a:1,x:"She couldn't remember anything about the film because she had been scrolling the whole time."},{q:"How did the writer's friends react to her plan?",o:["They thought it would make an interesting article.","They offered to join the experiment.","They were unhappy about the inconvenience it would cause them.","They doubted she would be able to finish it."],a:2,x:"They were less enthusiastic ‘mainly because they would now have to phone me’."},{q:"What does the writer say about the first week?",o:["She realised she depended on her phone more than she'd thought.","She was surprised by how quickly she adapted.","She missed receiving news updates.","She enjoyed exploring unfamiliar parts of the city."],a:0,x:"She kept reaching for the phone, got lost without maps and couldn't stand still."},{q:"Why does the writer mention the man who runs the newsagent's?",o:["to show how rude she had been to strangers","to give an example of how she began to connect with people around her","to explain where she bought her books","to suggest that other people were changing their habits too"],a:1,x:"After years of a distracted ‘thanks’, she now has long conversations with him."},{q:"What does the writer say about sleeping better?",o:["It was the main benefit of the experiment.","It only lasted for the second week.","It was the result of reading before bed.","It may not have been caused by giving up her phone."],a:3,x:"‘I can't be sure whether that was due to the experiment or… going to bed earlier out of boredom.’"},{q:"How does the writer feel about the experiment now?",o:["She thinks everyone should try giving up their phone.","She regrets going back to her smartphone.","She believes it has changed her habits, though it hasn't solved everything.","She considers it to have been a failure."],a:2,x:"She uses the phone differently now but is ‘not claiming to have found the answer’."}]},6:{title:"The classroom without walls",intro:"an article about a primary school that teaches many of its lessons outdoors",paras:["At Brackenfield Primary School, the day doesn't begin with children sitting at desks. Instead, whatever the weather, pupils put on waterproof trousers and boots and head into the woods behind the school. {1} On a typical morning, they might measure the height of trees for a maths lesson or write poems about what they can hear.","The idea came from the head teacher, Sarah Collins, who visited schools in Denmark where outdoor learning is common. {2} ‘The children were so calm and focused,’ she remembers. ‘I came home determined to try something similar.’","Not everyone was convinced at first. Some parents worried that their children would fall behind in reading and writing, while others were concerned about safety. {3} They were invited to spend a morning in the woods themselves, and most left with a very different opinion.","Five years on, the results have been encouraging. Test scores have risen slightly, but teachers say the biggest changes are harder to measure. {4} Children who struggled to sit still in a classroom often thrive outdoors, where they can move around freely.","There are, of course, practical difficulties. In winter it gets dark early, and on the coldest days lessons have to be shorter. {5} Collins, however, is unapologetic. ‘There's no such thing as bad weather,’ she says, ‘only the wrong clothes.’","Other schools in the area are now showing an interest, and several head teachers have visited Brackenfield to see the approach for themselves. {6} ‘Every school has some outdoor space,’ says Collins. ‘Even a small playground can become a place to learn.’"],options:["Rather than simply trying to reassure them, the school decided to show them what it meant.","Not all of them have woodland nearby, but that, she believes, shouldn't stop anyone.","She was struck by how different the atmosphere was from what she was used to.","Behaviour has improved, and pupils seem more confident about trying new things.","As a result, the school has decided to stop teaching reading and writing indoors.","Here, in a clearing surrounded by oak trees, they spend at least half of every school day.","Staff also need special training, and the waterproof clothing is expensive to replace."],key:[5,2,0,3,6,1],x:["F: ‘Here, in a clearing’ refers to ‘the woods behind the school’, and ‘On a typical morning’ continues the description.","C: ‘how different the atmosphere was’ is explained by ‘The children were so calm and focused’.","A: ‘reassure them’ refers to the worried parents; ‘show them’ is explained by inviting them into the woods.","D: improved behaviour and confidence are the changes that are ‘harder to measure’. E contradicts the rest of the text.","G: ‘also’ adds more practical difficulties to the dark winters and short lessons.","B: ‘them’ refers to the other schools, and ‘Every school has some outdoor space’ answers the woodland problem."]},7:{title:"Not your average job",intro:"an article in which four people talk about their unusual jobs",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Ella, ice-cream taster",text:["People assume my job is one long holiday, but tasting ice cream for a living is more scientific than it sounds. I work for a manufacturer, and I test every new flavour for texture, sweetness and how the taste changes as it melts. I can taste up to sixty samples a day, so I only ever take a tiny spoonful and I rinse my mouth with water between each one. The strangest part is that I've had to give up spicy food and coffee, because they affect my sense of taste. I got the job after studying food science at university, and it still amazes me that I'm paid to do something so many people would happily do for free."]},{name:"Rob, golf-ball diver",text:["I collect golf balls from the lakes on golf courses. The clubs pay me to clear them out, and I clean the balls and sell them back to players at a lower price. The water is usually cold and completely dark, so I work mostly by touch, which took a lot of practice. It isn't dangerous if you're careful, although I once came face to face with a very large and very angry fish, which gave me quite a fright. What I love is the independence – I choose my own hours and I'm my own boss. The downside is that the work depends entirely on the weather; in a wet winter, I can go weeks without earning anything."]},{name:"Maya, video game tester",text:["Before I started testing video games, I thought I'd found the perfect job for someone who spent every free moment gaming. The reality is quite different. I might play the same level two hundred times, trying to make the game crash – walking into walls or pressing buttons in strange combinations. It can be incredibly repetitive, and by Friday I usually don't want to see a screen at all. Still, there's real satisfaction in finding a problem nobody else has noticed. And when a game I've worked on is released and gets good reviews, I feel proud, even though my name is buried somewhere near the end of the credits where nobody reads it."]},{name:"Sam, dog walker",text:["I left a well-paid job in finance to become a dog walker, and my friends thought I'd lost my mind. I walk around fifteen dogs a day in groups of four, so you need to be well organised and understand how different dogs behave together. I've had to learn a lot about animal behaviour, which I studied through an online course. I earn less than I did at the bank, but I'm outside all day, I'm much fitter, and I no longer lie awake worrying about work. My only real complaint is that some clients treat what I do as a hobby rather than a proper business, and forget to pay on time."]}],qs:[{q:"has had to change their diet because of their work?",a:0,x:"Ella gave up spicy food and coffee because they affect her sense of taste."},{q:"admits that their job can be boring?",a:2,x:"Maya: ‘It can be incredibly repetitive’."},{q:"had to develop a skill to cope with not being able to see?",a:1,x:"Rob: the water is dark, ‘so I work mostly by touch, which took a lot of practice’."},{q:"describes a moment when they were frightened?",a:1,x:"Rob met ‘a very angry fish, which gave me quite a fright’."},{q:"studied to gain knowledge they needed after starting the job?",a:3,x:"Sam learned about animal behaviour ‘through an online course’."},{q:"feels that their contribution goes largely unnoticed?",a:2,x:"Maya's name is ‘buried… where nobody reads it’."},{q:"says some customers don't take their business seriously?",a:3,x:"Sam: clients ‘treat what I do as a hobby rather than a proper business’."},{q:"mentions a qualification that led to their job?",a:0,x:"Ella got the job ‘after studying food science at university’."},{q:"has an income that depends on conditions outside their control?",a:1,x:"Rob: ‘in a wet winter, I can go weeks without earning anything’."},{q:"says the job has improved their well-being?",a:3,x:"Sam is ‘much fitter’ and no longer lies ‘awake worrying about work’."}]}}}),LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t4",title:"Test 4",topics:"Why we like being scared · Juggling · E-bikes · A teenage chess champion · A clockmaker · Keeping fit",parts:{1:{title:"Why we love to be scared",text:`Every October, millions of people queue for hours at haunted houses or sit through horror films. It might seem strange to {0} for something that makes you feel afraid, but psychologists say there is a good reason for it.

When we are scared, our bodies {1} a chemical called adrenaline, which makes the heart beat faster and gives us a sudden {2} of energy. If we know that we are actually {3} no danger, this reaction can be surprisingly enjoyable. Many people describe a feeling of relief, and even happiness, once the fear has {4}.

Researchers have also found that watching a scary film with friends can {5} people closer together. Sharing a frightening experience seems to create a strong sense of connection.

However, some people simply can't {6} up with the tension and find horror films genuinely upsetting. In the end, it all {7} down to personal taste – and there is nothing wrong with {8} for a comedy instead.`,example:{o:["pay","spend","give","charge"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["let","issue","release","free"],a:2,x:"The body releases a chemical or hormone. ‘Let’ needs ‘out’, ‘issue’ is for documents or statements, and ‘free’ means give someone their freedom."},{o:["blast","burst","crash","explosion"],a:1,x:"Collocation: a (sudden) burst of energy. ‘A blast of’ is used for air or music."},{o:["at","in","on","under"],a:1,x:"Fixed phrase: be in danger / in no danger."},{o:["run out","ended up","gone by","passed"],a:3,x:"A feeling passes = it goes away. ‘Gone by’ is used for time, ‘run out’ for supplies."},{o:["bring","take","carry","hold"],a:0,x:"bring people (closer) together = make their relationship stronger."},{o:["keep","put","come","make"],a:1,x:"put up with = tolerate. ‘Keep up with’ means stay at the same level; ‘come up with’ means think of an idea."},{o:["goes","gets","comes","turns"],a:2,x:"it (all) comes down to = the most important factor is."},{o:["choosing","deciding","selecting","opting"],a:3,x:"opt FOR something. choose and select take a direct object; decide takes ON."}]},2:{title:"A skill for life: juggling",text:`Juggling looks impossible to most people, but the basics can be learned in {0} than a week. All you need is three soft balls and a {1} of patience.

Beginners usually start with just one ball, throwing it from hand to hand in a gentle arc. {2} this feels comfortable, a second ball is added. The key is to look not at your hands but at the top of each throw. It is surprisingly difficult at first, and you will spend a lot of time picking balls {3} off the floor.

Apart {4} being fun, juggling seems to have real benefits for the brain. In one study, adults {5} practised for three months showed changes in the areas of the brain that deal with movement and vision. {6} is more, these changes appeared regardless of how good the jugglers actually became.

So if you are looking for a new hobby, why not give it a try? It costs very little, and you can practise {7} you have a few spare minutes. You have nothing to lose – apart, {8} course, from your dignity!`,example:{a:"less"},gaps:[{a:["bit","lot"],x:"a bit of / a lot of + uncountable noun (patience)."},{a:["once","when","after"],x:"Time linker: once this feels comfortable = as soon as."},{a:["up"],x:"pick something up = lift it from the floor."},{a:["from"],x:"apart from + -ing = as well as."},{a:["who","that"],x:"Defining relative clause about people: adults who practised."},{a:["what"],x:"Fixed linker: What is more, … = in addition."},{a:["whenever","when","wherever"],x:"whenever = at any time that."},{a:["of"],x:"Fixed phrase: of course."}]},3:{title:"The rise of the electric bike",text:`Electric bikes have become {0} popular in recent years.
Their {1} is easy to understand: they allow riders to travel
long distances without getting {2}.
For many commuters they are a {3} alternative to the car,
and they produce far less {4} than petrol vehicles.

Critics, however, point out that e-bikes are {5} expensive,
and that their batteries raise {6} questions about waste.
There are also {7} about safety, as some riders travel at high speed.
Even so, most experts agree that e-bikes will play an {8} role in the future of transport.`,example:{w:"INCREASE",a:"increasingly"},gaps:[{w:"POPULAR",a:["popularity"],x:"Noun after the possessive ‘their’: their popularity."},{w:"EXHAUST",a:["exhausted"],x:"get + adjective describing a feeling: get exhausted (not exhausting)."},{w:"PRACTICE",a:["practical"],x:"Adjective before ‘alternative’: a practical alternative."},{w:"POLLUTE",a:["pollution"],x:"Uncountable noun after ‘far less’."},{w:"REASON",a:["unreasonably"],x:"Adverb before an adjective, with a negative prefix because critics are complaining: unreasonably expensive."},{w:"ENVIRONMENT",a:["environmental"],x:"Adjective before ‘questions’."},{w:"WORRY",a:["worries"],x:"Plural noun after ‘There are also’."},{w:"IMPORTANCE",a:["important"],x:"Adjective after ‘an’ and before ‘role’: an important role."}]},4:{items:[{s1:"I'm sure Tom didn't break the window on purpose.",key:"CAN'T",pre:"Tom",post:"the window on purpose.",a:[["cannot have"],["broken"]],x:"Certain it didn't happen: can't have + past participle."},{s1:"They're building a new stadium near the river.",key:"BEING",pre:"A new stadium",post:"near the river.",a:[["is being"],["built"]],x:"Present continuous passive: is being + past participle."},{s1:"It took me two hours to finish the report.",key:"SPENT",pre:"I",post:"the report.",a:[["spent two hours"],["finishing","completing","writing","doing"]],x:"spend + time + -ing."},{s1:"Jane regrets selling her old car.",key:"WISHES",pre:"Jane",post:"her old car.",a:[["wishes she had not"],["sold"]],x:"Regret about the past: wish + past perfect (hadn't = had not, two words)."},{s1:"‘You should take an umbrella,’ my mum told me.",key:"ADVISED",pre:"My mum",post:"an umbrella.",a:[["advised me"],["to take","to bring"]],x:"advise somebody to do something."},{s1:"Nobody told us that the museum was closed.",key:"NOT",pre:"We",post:"that the museum was closed.",a:[["were not"],["told","informed"]],x:"Passive: Nobody told us → We were not told."}]},5:{title:"Checkmate at fifteen",intro:"an article about a teenage chess champion",paras:["When fifteen-year-old Aisha Karim sat down opposite her opponent at the national junior chess final last spring, she had a secret: she had only been playing seriously for three years. Most of the other finalists had been taking lessons since they could barely reach the table. ‘I remember looking around the room and thinking, everyone here knows something I don't,’ she says. ‘It was only when I won my first game that I realised they were just as nervous as me.’","Aisha discovered chess almost by accident. During a long stay in hospital after breaking her leg, she was given an old chess set by the boy in the next bed. With nothing else to do, the two of them played for hours every day. ‘He beat me about forty times in a row,’ she laughs. ‘I was so annoyed that I started watching videos about openings just so I could win once before he went home.’","Back at school, she joined the chess club, where the teacher in charge, Mr Doyle, quickly noticed her talent. Rather than simply entering her for competitions, he encouraged her to analyse every game she lost. ‘He made me write down the moment when things started going wrong,’ Aisha explains. ‘At first I hated it. Now I think it's the most useful thing anyone has taught me – and not just for chess.’","Her rapid progress has not come without sacrifices. She practises for around two hours each evening, which leaves little time for other activities, and she admits that some friendships have suffered. ‘A few people think I'm a bit obsessed,’ she says. ‘Maybe they're right. But when I care about something, I can't do it half-heartedly.’","What she finds most frustrating is the assumption, still common in the chess world, that girls are less likely to succeed. At tournaments, she has occasionally been mistaken for someone's younger sister rather than a competitor. Rather than getting angry, she tries to let her results speak for themselves, and she now helps run a weekly club for girls at her local library.","Aisha is careful not to make grand predictions about her future. She would like to reach international level, but she is equally keen to study medicine. ‘Chess has taught me that plans change,’ she says. ‘Three years ago I didn't even know how the knight moved. Who knows what I'll be doing in another three?’"],qs:[{q:"How did Aisha feel at the start of the national final?",o:["confident because she had prepared well","worried that she was less experienced than the others","relieved that her opponents seemed nervous","surprised to have reached the final so quickly"],a:1,x:"‘Everyone here knows something I don't’ – she had played for only three years. She only noticed the others' nerves after her first win (C)."},{q:"Why did Aisha start watching videos about chess openings?",o:["She wanted to enter competitions.","She had nothing else to do in hospital.","She was determined to beat the boy in the next bed.","A doctor suggested chess as a hobby."],a:2,x:"‘I was so annoyed that I started watching videos… just so I could win once before he went home.’ Boredom explains why they played (B), not why she studied."},{q:"What does Aisha say about Mr Doyle's approach?",o:["It helped her win competitions straight away.","She disliked it at first but now values it beyond chess.","It was very different from other teachers' methods.","She still finds it frustrating."],a:1,x:"‘At first I hated it. Now I think it's the most useful thing… and not just for chess.’"},{q:"What does Aisha suggest about herself in paragraph 4?",o:["She regrets losing touch with some friends.","She would like to spend less time practising.","She thinks people misunderstand chess.","She puts all her effort into things she cares about."],a:3,x:"‘When I care about something, I can't do it half-heartedly.’ She mentions friendships suffering but doesn't say she regrets it (A)."},{q:"How does Aisha deal with people who doubt that girls can succeed at chess?",o:["She lets her results show what she can do.","She complains to tournament organisers.","She avoids tournaments where this happens.","She tells other girls to challenge those people."],a:0,x:"‘She tries to let her results speak for themselves.’ The girls' club supports girls; it isn't about arguing (D)."},{q:"What is Aisha's attitude to her future?",o:["She is sure she will become a doctor.","She is worried about having to choose between her interests.","She is open to her plans changing.","She is confident of reaching international level."],a:2,x:"‘Chess has taught me that plans change… Who knows what I'll be doing?’"}]},6:{title:"Keeping time",intro:"an article about a man who repairs antique clocks",paras:["In a narrow workshop at the end of a quiet street in York, dozens of clocks tick at slightly different speeds. The sound would drive most people mad, but Peter Hale barely notices it any more. {1} ‘People think it must be relaxing,’ he says. ‘Actually, when they all strike twelve together, it's deafening.’","Peter was trained by his father, who ran the business before him. As a boy he spent his Saturdays sorting tiny screws into boxes and watching his father work. {2} ‘I thought it was the most boring job in the world,’ he admits. ‘I wanted to be a footballer.’","It was only after an injury ended his hopes of a sporting career that he returned to the workshop. {3} By the end of his first year back, he had repaired a clock entirely on his own – a battered kitchen clock that still hangs above his desk.","Today most of his customers bring him clocks that have been in their families for generations. Repairs can take months, partly because replacement parts no longer exist. {4} ‘You can't just order a new wheel for a clock made in 1790,’ he explains. ‘You have to make it yourself.’","The emotional value of the clocks means the job is about more than mechanics. Customers sometimes cry when they hear a clock working for the first time in decades. {5} ‘It reminded her of her grandparents' house,’ Peter says. ‘That's when you realise you're not just fixing a machine.’","Peter worries that the skills he learned may soon disappear. Very few young people are training as clockmakers, and courses are hard to find. {6} So far, two apprentices have completed their training in his workshop, and a third starts next month."],options:["In many cases, Peter has to create the missing pieces by hand.","Determined to do something about it, he now offers to teach anyone with a genuine interest.","Modern digital clocks, on the other hand, rarely need repairing at all.","He has been repairing antique clocks here for almost forty years.","To his surprise, he found the work far more absorbing than he remembered.","He remembers one elderly woman who had to sit down when her family's clock began to chime.","At the time, however, he had no intention of following in his father's footsteps."],key:[3,6,4,0,5,1],x:["D: ‘here for almost forty years’ explains why he ‘barely notices’ the ticking any more.","G: ‘At the time, however’ contrasts his childhood in the workshop with not wanting the job – ‘I wanted to be a footballer.’","E: returning after his injury, he was surprised to enjoy it, and by the end of the year he had repaired a clock alone.","A: ‘create the missing pieces by hand’ connects with ‘You have to make it yourself.’ C doesn't fit – the paragraph is about antique clocks.","F: ‘one elderly woman’ is the ‘her’ in ‘It reminded her of her grandparents' house’.","B: ‘do something about it’ refers to the disappearing skills, and leads to the apprentices he has trained."]},7:{title:"How I keep fit",intro:"an article in which four people describe their favourite way of keeping fit",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Nina",text:["I started swimming in the sea every morning when the pools were closed a few years ago, and I've never stopped – even in January. The first few times, the cold took my breath away and I lasted about two minutes. Now I stay in for twenty. People assume I must be incredibly tough, but honestly, the hardest part is getting out of bed, not getting into the water. I swim with a group of women I'd never have met otherwise; we range from nineteen to seventy-two. Afterwards I feel wide awake and ready for anything, which is more than coffee ever did for me."]},{name:"Kofi",text:["I'd always found gyms boring – running on a machine while staring at a wall just isn't me. A colleague persuaded me to try the climbing centre near our office, and I was hooked immediately, because every route is like a puzzle you solve with your body. I'm not naturally strong, so at first I had to rely on technique, which turned out to be an advantage. The only downside is the cost: membership isn't cheap, and I've had to cut back on eating out to afford it. I've also hurt my shoulder twice by trying routes that were too hard for me."]},{name:"Rosa",text:["I took up salsa at fifty-five, after my children left home and the house felt far too quiet. I was terrified at my first class because everyone else seemed to know what they were doing, but the teacher paired me with patient partners and I soon relaxed. What I didn't expect was how much it would help my memory – learning the sequences keeps my brain as busy as my feet. I now go three times a week, and my doctor says my blood pressure is the best it's been for years, which I put down entirely to dancing."]},{name:"Liam",text:["I cycle eighteen kilometres to work and back, which sounds impressive, but really I started because I was fed up with sitting in traffic. It's actually quicker than driving at rush hour, and I save a fortune on fuel and parking. I don't think of it as exercise, which is probably why I stick to it – it's just how I get to work. The worst thing is arriving soaked on rainy days, although the office has showers now, after a few of us asked for them. I've lost six kilos without even trying."]}],qs:[{q:"started their activity mainly for practical reasons?",a:3,x:"Liam was ‘fed up with sitting in traffic’; cycling is quicker and cheaper."},{q:"mentions an unexpected benefit for the mind?",a:2,x:"Rosa: ‘What I didn't expect was how much it would help my memory.’"},{q:"has had to spend less on something else because of the activity?",a:1,x:"Kofi has ‘had to cut back on eating out to afford it’."},{q:"says other people misunderstand what is difficult about the activity?",a:0,x:"Nina: people think she's tough, but ‘the hardest part is getting out of bed’."},{q:"was introduced to the activity by someone they work with?",a:1,x:"Kofi: ‘A colleague persuaded me to try the climbing centre.’"},{q:"says a change at work has made the activity more pleasant?",a:3,x:"Liam: ‘the office has showers now, after a few of us asked for them.’"},{q:"took up the activity after a change in their family situation?",a:2,x:"Rosa started ‘after my children left home and the house felt far too quiet’."},{q:"has got to know people of very different ages?",a:0,x:"Nina's group ranges ‘from nineteen to seventy-two’."},{q:"found that a personal weakness became an advantage?",a:1,x:"Kofi isn't naturally strong, so he relied on technique, ‘which turned out to be an advantage’."},{q:"links the activity to an improvement in a specific health measurement?",a:2,x:"Rosa's doctor says ‘my blood pressure is the best it's been for years’."}]}}}),LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t5",title:"Test 5",topics:"Vinyl records · Night owls · Learning an instrument · Life on a boat · A library of things · Young entrepreneurs",parts:{1:{title:"The return of vinyl",text:`Not long ago, vinyl records seemed to be {0} for extinction. CDs and then streaming services had {1} them almost completely, and many record shops were forced to close.

Today, however, vinyl is enjoying a remarkable comeback. Sales have risen every year for more than a decade, and a {2} number of buyers are teenagers who were born long after records went out of {3}.

So what lies {4} this revival? For some fans, it is the sound, which they describe as warmer than digital music. For others, it is the pleasure of owning something physical: a large cover to look at, and a record that has to be {5} over halfway through.

Not everyone is convinced, though. Records are expensive, easily damaged and {6} up a lot of space. Surveys also {7} that many young buyers never actually play their records. For them, it seems, vinyl is less about music and more about making a {8} about who they are.`,example:{o:["heading","going","moving","travelling"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["exchanged","replaced","switched","substituted"],a:1,x:"replace something = take its place. ‘Substitute’ needs FOR (substitute A for B); exchange and switch need a second thing."},{o:["raising","lifting","growing","gaining"],a:2,x:"a growing number of = more and more. ‘Raising’ needs an object (raise money)."},{o:["trend","mode","taste","fashion"],a:3,x:"Fixed phrase: go out of fashion."},{o:["beneath","behind","under","below"],a:1,x:"What lies behind something? = what is the real reason for it."},{o:["turned","rolled","switched","changed"],a:0,x:"turn a record over = put the other side up."},{o:["make","get","take","put"],a:2,x:"take up space = fill or use space."},{o:["propose","advise","recommend","suggest"],a:3,x:"Research suggests that = shows or indicates. propose, advise and recommend are about giving ideas or advice."},{o:["statement","declaration","announcement","claim"],a:0,x:"make a statement (about something) = show your identity or opinions through what you do or wear."}]},2:{title:"Night owls and early birds",text:`Some people leap out of bed at six o'clock full of energy, {0} others can barely function before ten. Scientists now know that {1} of these habits are largely decided {2} our genes rather than by laziness or discipline.

Everyone has an internal body clock that controls {3} sleepy or alert we feel at different times of day. In ‘early birds’ this clock runs slightly ahead; in ‘night owls’ it runs behind. {4} a result, forcing a night owl to start work at eight is a bit {5} asking an early bird to stay up until two in the morning.

The problem is that modern society is organised largely around early risers. Schools and offices open early, and people who struggle to get up are often seen as lacking motivation. {6} is slowly changing, however. A number of companies now let staff choose their own hours, {7} long as the work gets done.

So if you are a night owl, don't feel guilty. Studies suggest that you are just as likely to succeed as an early bird – if not more {8}.`,example:{a:"while"},gaps:[{a:["both"],x:"both of these (two) habits."},{a:["by"],x:"Passive agent: decided BY our genes (parallel with ‘by laziness’)."},{a:["how"],x:"how + adjective: how sleepy or alert we feel."},{a:["as"],x:"Fixed linker: as a result."},{a:["like"],x:"be like + -ing = be similar to."},{a:["this","that"],x:"Pronoun referring back to the whole situation: This is slowly changing."},{a:["as","so"],x:"as long as = on condition that. (‘So long as’ is also correct.)"},{a:["so"],x:"if not more so = possibly even more (successful)."}]},3:{title:"Why learn an instrument?",text:`Learning to play an instrument has many {0} benefits.
Research shows that children who play music often do better in {1} tests.
Playing requires {2} concentration, which trains the brain to focus,
and playing in a band or orchestra can improve {3} skills.

For adults, music can be a {4} way to relax after work.
However, many beginners give up because progress feels {5} slow.
Teachers say the key is to set {6} goals
and to practise {7} rather than for hours once a week.
With enough {8}, almost anyone can learn to play well.`,example:{w:"SURPRISE",a:"surprising"},gaps:[{w:"ACADEMY",a:["academic"],x:"Adjective before ‘tests’: academic tests."},{w:"CONTINUE",a:["continuous"],x:"Adjective before ‘concentration’: continuous concentration (without stopping)."},{w:"COMMUNICATE",a:["communication"],x:"Noun used like an adjective: communication skills."},{w:"WONDER",a:["wonderful"],x:"Adjective after ‘a’ and before ‘way’."},{w:"FRUSTRATE",a:["frustratingly"],x:"Adverb modifying the adjective ‘slow’: frustratingly slow."},{w:"REAL",a:["realistic"],x:"Adjective before ‘goals’: realistic goals."},{w:"REGULAR",a:["regularly"],x:"Adverb describing how you practise."},{w:"PATIENT",a:["patience"],x:"Uncountable noun after ‘enough’."}]},4:{items:[{s1:"The last time I went skiing was five years ago.",key:"BEEN",pre:"I",post:"for five years.",a:[["have not been"],["skiing"]],x:"The last time… was five years ago = I haven't been / done it for five years."},{s1:"Could you help me move this table?",key:"MIND",pre:"Would",post:"move this table?",a:[["you mind"],["helping me"]],x:"Would you mind + -ing?"},{s1:"Paula didn't go to the party because she was tired.",key:"IF",pre:"Paula would have gone to the party",post:"tired.",a:[["if she had not"],["been"]],x:"Third conditional: if + past perfect (hadn't been)."},{s1:"People believe the thief escaped through a window.",key:"BELIEVED",pre:"The thief",post:"through a window.",a:[["is believed"],["to have escaped"]],x:"Passive reporting with a past action: is believed + to have + past participle."},{s1:"There were fewer people at the concert than we expected.",key:"AS",pre:"There",post:"at the concert as we expected.",a:[["were not as"],["many people"]],x:"fewer … than = not as many … as."},{s1:"Sam started playing the drums when he was ten.",key:"SINCE",pre:"Sam",post:"he was ten.",a:[["has played"],["the drums since"]],x:"Present perfect + since + point in time."}]},5:{title:"Home is where the boat is",intro:"an article about a family who moved onto a canal boat",paras:["Three years ago, Hannah and Joe Price sold their three-bedroom house in Bristol and moved, with their two young children, onto a narrowboat just two metres wide. Friends told them they were mad. ‘My mother cried,’ Hannah remembers. ‘She was convinced the children would fall into the canal on the first day.’","The decision was not as sudden as it looked. For years the couple had been working long hours to pay a mortgage they could barely afford, and they saw their children for little more than an hour a day. ‘We weren't unhappy, exactly,’ says Joe. ‘We were just tired all the time. One evening we worked out what we'd have left if we sold the house, and it was the first time in years we'd felt excited about anything.’","Life on the water took some getting used to. Everything, from washing clothes to charging a phone, requires planning. Water has to be collected from taps along the canal, and in winter the boat's small stove is the only source of heat. ‘The first January was a real test,’ Hannah admits. ‘There were mornings when there was ice on the inside of the windows. If we'd moved in winter instead of summer, I'm not sure we'd have stayed.’","The children, now eight and ten, have adapted faster than their parents. They are taught at home for part of the year while the family travels, and join a local school when the boat stays in one place during the winter months. Hannah was worried that they would fall behind, but their teachers report that they are confident, curious and unusually good at practical tasks.","Money is still tight, but in a different way. Both parents now work part-time – Joe repairs engines for other boat owners and Hannah writes for travel websites – and their living costs are a fraction of what they were. What they have gained, they say, is time. ‘We eat every meal together,’ says Joe. ‘That alone makes it worth it.’","They are realistic about the future. As the children grow, two metres of space will start to feel very small, and the couple accept that they may eventually return to living on land. ‘We don't see it as a permanent choice,’ says Hannah. ‘It's a chapter, not the whole story. But it's a chapter none of us will forget.’"],qs:[{q:"How did other people react when the family moved onto the boat?",o:["They offered practical advice.","They were jealous of the family's freedom.","They thought it was a foolish decision.","They supported the plan."],a:2,x:"‘Friends told them they were mad’ and Hannah's mother cried."},{q:"What led the couple to sell their house?",o:["They were very unhappy in their jobs.","They realised a different kind of life might be possible.","Their children wanted to live somewhere new.","They could no longer pay their mortgage."],a:1,x:"Working out what they'd have left made them ‘excited about anything’ for the first time. They could ‘barely’ afford the mortgage – not ‘no longer’ (D) – and ‘weren't unhappy, exactly’ (A)."},{q:"What does Hannah say about their first winter?",o:["They might have given up if they had moved in winter.","It was easier than she had expected.","It made the family move back onto land for a while.","It taught them how to plan their water supply."],a:0,x:"‘If we'd moved in winter instead of summer, I'm not sure we'd have stayed.’"},{q:"What do the children's teachers say about them?",o:["They have fallen behind in some subjects.","They find it hard to settle into school.","They prefer being taught at home.","They have developed some valuable qualities."],a:3,x:"They are ‘confident, curious and unusually good at practical tasks’. Falling behind was only Hannah's worry (A)."},{q:"What is the main benefit of their new life, according to paragraph 5?",o:["They earn more money.","They spend more time together as a family.","They have more interesting jobs.","They see new places."],a:1,x:"‘What they have gained… is time. We eat every meal together.’ Money is ‘still tight’ (A)."},{q:"How does Hannah feel about the future?",o:["She expects them to stay on the boat permanently.","She regrets that they will have to leave the boat.","She sees boat life as one stage in the family's life.","She doubts whether it has been worthwhile."],a:2,x:"‘It's a chapter, not the whole story.’"}]},6:{title:"Borrow, don't buy",intro:"an article about a shop where people borrow household items instead of buying them",paras:["When Marta Silva needed a drill to put up a single shelf, she was about to buy one when she stopped to think. The average drill, she had read, is used for just thirteen minutes in its entire life. {1} Two years later, that thought has become the Library of Things, a small shop in east London where members can borrow over four hundred everyday items for a few pounds.","The collection includes almost anything people need occasionally but rarely: tents, sewing machines, carpet cleaners and even a chocolate fountain. {2} ‘The pressure washer is booked every weekend from April to October,’ Marta laughs. ‘People clearly have a lot of patios.’","Setting up the library was harder than Marta expected. She had assumed that people would happily donate items they no longer wanted, and many did. {3} ‘We were given a lot of things that simply didn't work,’ she says. ‘One man brought us a lawnmower with no engine.’","To solve the problem, the library now checks and repairs everything before it goes on the shelves. Volunteers run the repair sessions on Tuesday evenings. {4} Several of them say that fixing things has given them a new sense of purpose.","Members are asked to return items clean and on time, and the vast majority do. {5} Marta believes this is because borrowers feel part of a community rather than customers of a business. ‘People don't want to let their neighbours down,’ she explains.","The idea is now spreading. Similar libraries have opened in other cities, and Marta regularly advises groups who want to start their own. {6} ‘Every drill we lend is one less drill made, bought and eventually thrown away,’ she says. ‘It all adds up.’"],options:["Not all of the donations were useful, however.","For her, the environmental argument is the most important one.","It seemed absurd to spend money on something that would sit in a cupboard for years.","Many of them are retired engineers and electricians.","Anyone who breaks an item has to pay a large fine.","Some items turn out to be far more popular than others.","In fact, in two years only a handful of items have gone missing."],key:[2,5,0,3,6,1],x:["C: thirteen minutes of use makes buying a drill seem absurd – and ‘that thought’ in the next sentence refers back to it.","F: ‘far more popular than others’ is illustrated by the pressure washer being booked every weekend.","A: ‘however’ contrasts with ‘many did’, and leads to things that ‘simply didn't work’.","D: ‘Many of them’ refers to the volunteers, and ‘Several of them’ continues the idea.","G: ‘only a handful have gone missing’ supports ‘the vast majority do’. E contradicts the community spirit Marta describes.","B: ‘the environmental argument’ is explained by the quote about fewer drills being made and thrown away."]},7:{title:"Starting out",intro:"an article in which four young people describe starting their own business",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Mei, bakery",text:["I started baking cakes for friends' birthdays while I was at university, and people began offering to pay. By my final year I was spending more time in the kitchen than in the library, which my parents weren't thrilled about, so we made a deal: I'd finish my degree first. After graduating I rented a tiny kitchen by the hour. The hardest thing has been pricing. For ages I undercharged because I felt awkward asking for money, until a business adviser sat me down and showed me I was actually losing money on every wedding cake."]},{name:"Jonas, dog-walking app",text:["My app connects dog owners with local students who want to earn a bit of money walking dogs. The idea came from my own frustration: I'd started a job with long hours and had nobody to walk my dog. I had no technical background, so I taught myself to code using free online courses, which took almost a year. Getting the first hundred users was painfully slow. What changed everything was a short video one customer posted of her dog with its walker – it was shared thousands of times, and suddenly we had more requests than walkers."]},{name:"Farah, vintage clothes",text:["I've always loved old clothes, so selling them online felt natural. I buy at markets and charity shops, clean and repair everything, and photograph it all in my bedroom. At first I tried to do everything myself, including the accounts, and I was working until two in the morning. Now my sister handles the finances, which has been a huge relief. The best part is the environmental side – every jacket I sell is one less new one being made. I still don't make much profit, but I'd rather do something I believe in than something that just pays well."]},{name:"Ollie, mobile bike repairs",text:["I used to work in a bike shop, but I noticed that lots of people never got round to bringing their bikes in. So I bought an old van, fitted it out with tools and started going to people's homes and offices instead. Borrowing the money for the van was scary – I'd never had a loan before – but I paid it back within eighteen months. Winter is quiet because fewer people cycle, so during those months I run bike maintenance workshops for companies, which has become a big part of the business."]}],qs:[{q:"agreed to wait before starting their business properly?",a:0,x:"Mei made a deal with her parents to ‘finish my degree first’."},{q:"learned a necessary skill without paying for it?",a:1,x:"Jonas ‘taught myself to code using free online courses’."},{q:"has found a way to earn money when business is slow?",a:3,x:"Ollie runs workshops for companies during the quiet winter months."},{q:"receives help from a relative?",a:2,x:"Farah: ‘my sister handles the finances’."},{q:"says they did not ask for enough money at first?",a:0,x:"Mei ‘undercharged because I felt awkward asking for money’."},{q:"got the idea for the business from a problem of their own?",a:1,x:"Jonas had ‘nobody to walk my dog’."},{q:"felt anxious about owing money?",a:3,x:"Ollie: ‘Borrowing the money for the van was scary.’"},{q:"cares more about their principles than about profit?",a:2,x:"Farah would ‘rather do something I believe in than something that just pays well’."},{q:"grew quickly because of something a customer did?",a:1,x:"A customer's video ‘was shared thousands of times’."},{q:"changed where the service is provided to make it more convenient?",a:3,x:"Ollie goes ‘to people's homes and offices instead’."}]}}}),LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t6",title:"Test 6",topics:"Walking meetings · The sandwich · Urban gardens · The lighthouse · Cycling across Europe · Volunteers",parts:{1:{title:"Meetings on the move",text:`More and more companies are {0} their staff to hold meetings on foot instead of around a table. The idea is not new – several famous thinkers were known for their long walks – but it has {1} popularity as office workers spend more and more of their day sitting down.

Supporters say that walking meetings have several advantages. Moving around {2} blood flow to the brain, which may help people think more creatively. Walking side by side, rather than facing each other across a desk, can also make difficult conversations feel less {3}.

There are some practical problems, of course. It is hard to take notes while walking, and bad weather can quickly {4} a meeting. Experts recommend keeping walking meetings short, with no more than three people, so that everyone can {5} the discussion.

Perhaps the main thing that {6} people back is simply habit. Many of us feel that a ‘proper’ meeting has to take {7} in a meeting room. But as one manager put it, ‘Once you've tried it, sitting down for an hour feels like a {8} of time.’`,example:{o:["encouraging","suggesting","proposing","insisting"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["reached","raised","gained","arrived"],a:2,x:"Collocation: gain popularity. ‘Reach’ and ‘raise’ don't go with popularity; ‘arrive’ has no object."},{o:["grows","rises","expands","increases"],a:3,x:"increase + object: increases blood flow. grow, rise and expand are used without an object here."},{o:["intense","extreme","severe","acute"],a:0,x:"An intense conversation = emotional and serious. ‘Severe’ and ‘acute’ describe problems or pain."},{o:["damage","ruin","harm","injure"],a:1,x:"ruin an event = spoil it completely. damage, harm and injure are for objects, health or people."},{o:["chase","follow","trace","pursue"],a:1,x:"follow a discussion = understand it as it continues."},{o:["keeps","stops","puts","holds"],a:3,x:"hold somebody back = stop them from making progress."},{o:["part","room","place","space"],a:2,x:"take place = happen. ‘Take part’ means participate."},{o:["waste","loss","lack","spill"],a:0,x:"a waste of time = time used badly."}]},2:{title:"The story of the sandwich",text:`The sandwich is said to have {0} invented in the eighteenth century by John Montagu, the fourth Earl of Sandwich. According to the story, he was so busy playing cards that he did not want to stop for a proper meal. Instead, he asked for some meat to be served between two slices of bread, {1} that he could eat with one hand and keep playing with the other.

Whether or not the story is true, people had certainly been eating bread with fillings long {2} the Earl was born. What he did give the snack, however, was a name, and it quickly became fashionable {3} wealthy Londoners, {4} of whom began asking for ‘the same as Sandwich’.

Today, the sandwich is one of the {5} popular foods in the world. {6} are countless varieties, from a simple cheese sandwich to elaborate creations with a dozen ingredients.

Part of its success lies in {7} convenient it is. A sandwich can be made in minutes, carried anywhere and eaten without a knife and fork. It is hard to imagine a busy working lunch without {8}.`,example:{a:"been"},gaps:[{a:["so"],x:"so that = in order that (purpose)."},{a:["before"],x:"long before = a long time before."},{a:["among","amongst","with"],x:"fashionable among / with a group of people."},{a:["many","some","several","most"],x:"Quantifier + of whom in a non-defining relative clause: many of whom."},{a:["most"],x:"Superlative: one of the most popular foods."},{a:["there"],x:"There are countless varieties."},{a:["how"],x:"how + adjective after a preposition: lies in how convenient it is."},{a:["one"],x:"‘one’ replaces ‘a sandwich’ to avoid repetition."}]},3:{title:"Gardens in the sky",text:`Rooftop gardens are becoming a {0} sight in many cities.
For city dwellers, growing food is a {1} way to spend free time,
and watching seeds turn into vegetables brings real {2}.
Community gardens also bring together {3} who might never otherwise meet,
and reduce the {4} of local families on supermarkets.
Some projects even provide {5} for young people who have left school.

However, gardening in the city is not without its {6}.
Space is often {7} limited,
and plants on rooftops can be badly {8} to strong winds.`,example:{w:"FAMILY",a:"familiar"},gaps:[{w:"RELAX",a:["relaxing"],x:"Adjective describing the activity: a relaxing way (not relaxed)."},{w:"SATISFY",a:["satisfaction"],x:"Noun after ‘real’: bring satisfaction."},{w:"NEIGHBOUR",a:["neighbours","neighbors"],x:"Plural noun: people who live near each other."},{w:"DEPEND",a:["dependence","dependency"],x:"Noun after ‘the’ and before ‘of … on’."},{w:"EMPLOY",a:["employment"],x:"Uncountable noun after ‘provide’: provide employment."},{w:"DIFFICULT",a:["difficulties"],x:"Plural noun after ‘its’: not without its difficulties."},{w:"SEVERE",a:["severely"],x:"Adverb before an adjective: severely limited."},{w:"EXPOSE",a:["exposed"],x:"be exposed to something = not protected from it."}]},4:{items:[{s1:"The water was too cold for us to swim in.",key:"WARM",pre:"The water",post:"for us to swim in.",a:[["was not warm"],["enough"]],x:"too + adjective = not + opposite adjective + enough."},{s1:"‘I'll phone you when I arrive,’ Maria said to me.",key:"PROMISED",pre:"Maria",post:"when she arrived.",a:[["promised to"],["phone me","call me","ring me"]],x:"promise + to-infinitive."},{s1:"It's a pity I can't come to your wedding.",key:"WISH",pre:"I",post:"to your wedding.",a:[["wish i"],["could come"]],x:"Present regret: wish + could."},{s1:"Somebody stole Kate's bike last night.",key:"HAD",pre:"Kate",post:"last night.",a:[["had her bike"],["stolen"]],x:"have something done – used for bad experiences too: had her bike stolen."},{s1:"Someone needs to repair my phone.",key:"GET",pre:"I need",post:"repaired.",a:[["to get"],["my phone"]],x:"Causative: get something + past participle."},{s1:"Although it was expensive, they bought the house.",key:"EVEN",pre:"They bought the house",post:"expensive.",a:[["even though"],["it was"]],x:"even though + clause = although."}]},5:{title:"The key",intro:"an extract from a novel",kind:"extract",paras:["Leo had been at his aunt's house for exactly four hours when he decided that the summer was going to be a disaster. The village consisted of a harbour, a shop that closed at lunchtime and a lighthouse that no longer worked. There was no signal on his phone unless he stood at the end of the garden with one arm raised, which he had already done twice, feeling ridiculous.","His aunt Ruth did not seem to notice his mood – or if she did, she chose to ignore it. Over dinner she talked cheerfully about the tides and the seals and a woman in the village who made the best fish pie in Cornwall. Leo answered in single words. When she suggested a walk along the cliffs the next morning, he said he'd probably be too tired, and went upstairs before she could argue.","He was woken at six by an unfamiliar noise, a low rhythmic thumping from somewhere below his window. Pulling back the curtain, he saw Ruth in the garden, splitting logs with an axe. She was sixty-three years old. He watched for a long time, half expecting her to stop and rest, but she worked steadily until the pile was finished, then wiped her hands on her trousers and looked straight up at his window. She waved. Embarrassed, Leo let the curtain fall.","At breakfast she made no mention of it. Instead, she put a key on the table between them. It was large and old-fashioned, with a paper label tied to it. ‘The lighthouse,’ she said. ‘Your grandfather was the last keeper. Nobody's been up there for years. I'm too old for all those stairs these days, but I thought you might like to have a look.’","Leo turned the key over in his hands. He had heard vague stories about his grandfather, who had died before he was born, but he had never thought of him as a real person with a real job. ‘Is it safe?’ he asked, and immediately wished he hadn't, because it sounded as if he was scared.","Ruth considered the question seriously. ‘Probably,’ she said. ‘The top step's a bit loose. Your grandfather always said you could tell a lot about a person by whether they noticed it.’ She began clearing the plates. Leo put the key in his pocket, and for the first time since he had arrived, he found that he was looking forward to something."],qs:[{q:"What impression does the first paragraph give of the village?",o:["It is attractive but too busy.","It has very little to offer a teenager.","It is more modern than Leo expected.","It is friendly and welcoming."],a:1,x:"A harbour, a shop that closes at lunchtime, a lighthouse that doesn't work and almost no phone signal."},{q:"How did Ruth behave at dinner?",o:["She tried to find out why Leo was unhappy.","She was irritated by his short answers.","She stayed cheerful whatever his mood.","She insisted that he join her for a walk."],a:2,x:"She ‘did not seem to notice his mood – or… chose to ignore it’ and talked cheerfully. She didn't insist – Leo left ‘before she could argue’ (D)."},{q:"Why did Leo let the curtain fall?",o:["He felt awkward that Ruth had seen him watching her.","He was annoyed at being woken so early.","He wanted to go back to sleep.","He was worried that Ruth might hurt herself."],a:0,x:"She ‘looked straight up at his window. She waved. Embarrassed, Leo let the curtain fall.’"},{q:"Ruth's offer in paragraph 4 suggests that she",o:["wants Leo to repair the lighthouse.","is testing whether Leo is brave.","needs help with jobs she can no longer do.","hopes Leo will take an interest in his family's past."],a:3,x:"She tells him his grandfather was the last keeper and thinks he ‘might like to have a look’."},{q:"Why did Leo wish he hadn't asked ‘Is it safe?’",o:["He realised Ruth found the question rude.","He thought it made him sound frightened.","He knew the lighthouse had been checked recently.","He didn't want to go to the lighthouse."],a:1,x:"‘…because it sounded as if he was scared.’"},{q:"How has Leo's attitude changed by the end of the extract?",o:["He has begun to feel more positive about the summer.","He is still determined to go home.","He is nervous about the loose step.","He feels guilty about how he has treated Ruth."],a:0,x:"‘For the first time since he had arrived, he found that he was looking forward to something.’"}]},6:{title:"Across Europe by bike",intro:"an article about a young man who cycled from Portugal to the Arctic Circle",paras:["Last summer, nineteen-year-old Tom Ashworth cycled more than five thousand kilometres, from the southern tip of Portugal to the Arctic Circle in Finland. {1} ‘I'd only ever cycled to school and back,’ he admits. ‘I didn't even know how to fix a puncture.’","The idea came to him during his final year at school, when he was feeling unsure about what to do next. Most of his friends were going straight to university, but Tom wanted time to think. {2} His parents were less enthusiastic. ‘My mum spent the first month checking my location every hour,’ he laughs.","Tom spent six months preparing. He took a part-time job to pay for equipment and learned basic repairs by watching videos and practising on his old bike. {3} By the time he set off, he could ride a hundred kilometres a day without feeling exhausted.","The first weeks were the hardest. Spain was in the middle of a heatwave, and Tom often had to stop in the afternoon and wait for the temperature to drop. {4} ‘There was one day when I seriously thought about getting on a train home,’ he says. ‘I'm really glad I didn't.’","What kept him going was the kindness of strangers. In France, a farmer let him sleep in his barn during a thunderstorm. In Germany, a family he met at a campsite invited him to stay for three days. {5} ‘You hear so much bad news,’ he says, ‘but most people just want to help.’","Tom reached the Arctic Circle on the seventy-eighth day of his journey. {6} He has since started a degree in geography, and says the trip helped him choose it. ‘I spent three months looking at landscapes,’ he explains. ‘I realised I wanted to understand them.’"],options:["Twice his bike broke down miles from the nearest town.","What makes this more remarkable is that he had never been on a long bike ride before.","Experiences like these completely changed the way he thought about other people.","He had cycled the same route with his father two years earlier.","Standing beside the sign that marks the line, he felt proud rather than relieved.","A long journey seemed like the perfect way to do that.","He also built up his fitness gradually, cycling a little further each weekend."],key:[1,5,6,0,2,4],x:["B: ‘never been on a long bike ride before’ is explained by ‘I'd only ever cycled to school and back’. D contradicts this.","F: ‘to do that’ refers to wanting ‘time to think’.","G: ‘also’ adds fitness training to learning repairs, which explains how he could ride a hundred kilometres a day.","A: breakdowns far from towns, added to the heat, explain why he nearly took a train home.","C: ‘Experiences like these’ refers to the farmer and the German family, and leads to ‘most people just want to help’.","E: ‘the sign that marks the line’ refers to the Arctic Circle he has just reached."]},7:{title:"Giving time",intro:"an article in which four people talk about their volunteer work",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Grace, animal shelter",text:["I volunteer at an animal shelter every Saturday morning. People imagine I spend the whole time cuddling puppies, but most of the job is cleaning, feeding and walking dogs in the rain. I started because I wanted a dog of my own, but my landlord doesn't allow pets, so this seemed like the next best thing. The saddest part is when an older animal is overlooked again and again by families who want a young pet. Last month, though, a sixteen-year-old cat I'd been caring for finally found a home, and I actually cried."]},{name:"Daniel, hospital radio",text:["I present a two-hour show on our local hospital radio station every Thursday evening. Patients phone in with requests, and beforehand I visit the wards to chat and collect song choices. I'd always been quite shy, and the first time I spoke into the microphone my hands were shaking. Now I love it, and I think it's made me far more confident in meetings at work, too. The training was more thorough than I'd imagined – we had to learn all about the equipment and the rules about what we can and can't say on air."]},{name:"Priyanka, English classes",text:["I help teach English to adult refugees at a community centre two evenings a week. I'm not a qualified teacher, so I work alongside one, mostly helping students practise conversation. It can be emotionally demanding – some of them have been through terrible experiences – and the organisers are very good about checking that we're coping and offering support. What I love is how quickly the students progress when they have a real reason to learn. One man started with almost no English and now has a job interview next week."]},{name:"Harry, tree planting",text:["I joined a tree-planting group after I retired, mainly because I was spending far too much time indoors. We work on farms and in parks all over the county, usually in groups of about twenty. The physical work is much harder than I expected, and I ache for days afterwards, but I'm fitter now than I was at sixty. I like the fact that I'll never see most of these trees fully grown, but my grandchildren will. It's good to do something that will outlast me."]}],qs:[{q:"says volunteering has helped them in their job?",a:1,x:"Daniel: it has made him ‘far more confident in meetings at work’."},{q:"started volunteering because of a rule where they live?",a:0,x:"Grace's ‘landlord doesn't allow pets’."},{q:"works under the guidance of a professional?",a:2,x:"Priyanka is not qualified, ‘so I work alongside one’."},{q:"says the organisation makes sure volunteers are all right?",a:2,x:"The organisers are good ‘about checking that we're coping and offering support’."},{q:"found the work more physically demanding than expected?",a:3,x:"Harry: ‘much harder than I expected, and I ache for days afterwards’."},{q:"describes an emotional reaction to a happy outcome?",a:0,x:"Grace cried when the old cat ‘finally found a home’."},{q:"had to learn more before starting than they had imagined?",a:1,x:"Daniel: ‘The training was more thorough than I'd imagined.’"},{q:"likes knowing that future generations will benefit from their work?",a:3,x:"Harry: ‘my grandchildren will’ see the trees grown."},{q:"says people have the wrong idea about what the work involves?",a:0,x:"Grace: ‘People imagine I spend the whole time cuddling puppies.’"},{q:"mentions an improvement in their own fitness?",a:3,x:"Harry: ‘I'm fitter now than I was at sixty.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t7",title:"Test 7",topics:"Sleep and memory · The game of chess · Working from home · A month offline · The shark scientist · Young entrepreneurs",parts:{1:{title:"Why we sleep",text:`Most of us {0} sleep for granted, but scientists are only beginning to understand what happens to our brains while we rest.

One of the most surprising discoveries is that sleep has a direct {1} on memory. During the day, the brain {2} in huge amounts of information, but much of it is only stored {3} while we are asleep. In one study, volunteers who slept well after learning a list of words were far better at {4} them the next morning than those who had stayed awake all night.

Lack of sleep, on the other {5}, can leave us feeling irritable and more {6} to make mistakes at school or at work. It can even affect our appetite, making us want sugary snacks.

For these reasons, experts {7} teenagers to aim for between eight and ten hours a night, and to {8} bright screens for at least an hour before going to bed.`,example:{o:["take","make","keep","hold"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["result","effect","outcome","consequence"],a:1,x:"have an effect on something. Result, outcome and consequence aren't followed by ‘on’."},{o:["gets","brings","takes","holds"],a:2,x:"take in information = absorb it."},{o:["permanently","constantly","eternally","continually"],a:0,x:"stored permanently = kept for a long time. Constantly and continually mean ‘again and again’."},{o:["reminding","noticing","realising","recalling"],a:3,x:"recall = remember. ‘Remind’ needs a person: remind somebody of something."},{o:["side","hand","way","part"],a:1,x:"Fixed phrase: on the other hand."},{o:["possible","probable","likely","capable"],a:2,x:"be likely to do something. Possible and probable don't take a person + to-infinitive; capable is followed by ‘of’."},{o:["advise","suggest","propose","insist"],a:0,x:"advise somebody to do something. Suggest, propose and insist can't be followed by object + to-infinitive."},{o:["prevent","refuse","avoid","deny"],a:2,x:"avoid something = keep away from it. Prevent needs ‘from’; refuse and deny mean something different."}]},2:{title:"The game of kings",text:`Chess is one of the oldest board games in the world. It is thought to {0} originated in India around 1,500 years ago. {1} then, it has spread to almost every country on the planet, and today it is played by millions of people, both online {2} in clubs.

The rules have changed surprisingly little over the centuries. One exception is the queen, {3} was once one of the weakest pieces on the board but became the most powerful {4} the fifteenth century.

For a long time, people believed that no computer would ever be able to beat a human champion. That changed in 1997, {5} a machine called Deep Blue defeated the world champion, Garry Kasparov.

Although computers are now far stronger than any human player, this has not made the game {6} popular. On the contrary, many young players use computer programs to improve, and some reach an extraordinarily high level {7} the age of twelve. As one coach puts it, ‘It doesn't matter {8} old you are – you can always get better.’`,example:{a:"have"},gaps:[{a:["since"],x:"Since then + present perfect: it has spread."},{a:["and"],x:"both … and."},{a:["which"],x:"Non-defining relative clause after a comma – ‘that’ isn't possible here."},{a:["in","during"],x:"in / during the fifteenth century."},{a:["when"],x:"when introduces the time something happened: in 1997, when…"},{a:["less"],x:"has not made the game less popular = it is still popular."},{a:["by","before"],x:"by the age of twelve = not later than twelve."},{a:["how"],x:"It doesn't matter how old you are."}]},3:{title:"The kitchen-table office",text:`Working from home has become {0} common in recent years.
For many employees, the {1} to organise their own day is a big advantage.
They save time and money on travel, and many feel more {2} than in the office.
However, working alone can also lead to feelings of {3},
and some people find that the {4} between work and home life disappears.

Managers, meanwhile, often find it {5} to judge how hard people are working.
Experts say that a clear daily routine is {6}.
Taking a short walk at lunchtime can make a huge {7}
to how {8} people feel at the end of the day.`,example:{w:"INCREASE",a:"increasingly"},gaps:[{w:"FREE",a:["freedom"],x:"Noun after ‘the’: the freedom to organise their day."},{w:"PRODUCE",a:["productive"],x:"Adjective after ‘feel more’: productive."},{w:"LONELY",a:["loneliness"],x:"Noun after ‘feelings of’: loneliness."},{w:"SEPARATE",a:["separation"],x:"Noun after ‘the’: the separation between work and home."},{w:"POSSIBLE",a:["impossible"],x:"Negative adjective – the sense is that managers can't judge."},{w:"ESSENCE",a:["essential"],x:"Adjective after ‘is’: essential = extremely important."},{w:"DIFFER",a:["difference"],x:"make a difference to something."},{w:"ENERGY",a:["energetic"],x:"Adjective after ‘how’: how energetic people feel."}]},4:{items:[{s1:"I haven't seen my cousin for five years.",key:"LAST",pre:"I",post:"five years ago.",a:[["last saw"],["my cousin"]],x:"haven't done something for + time = last did something + time + ago."},{s1:"It wasn't necessary for you to bring any food, so you didn't.",key:"NEED",pre:"You",post:"any food, so you didn't.",a:[["did not need"],["to bring"]],x:"didn't need to = it wasn't necessary. The original says you did not bring food; needn't have brought would mean that you did."},{s1:"‘Why don't we go to the cinema?’ said Tom.",key:"SUGGESTED",pre:"Tom",post:"to the cinema.",a:[["suggested"],["going","that we go","that we should go","we go","we should go"]],x:"suggest + -ing, or suggest (that) + clause. Never suggest + to-infinitive."},{s1:"People say that the castle is haunted.",key:"SAID",pre:"The castle",post:"haunted.",a:[["is said"],["to be"]],x:"Impersonal passive: be said to be."},{s1:"I regret not studying harder at school.",key:"WISH",pre:"I",post:"harder at school.",a:[["wish i"],["had studied"]],x:"Regret about the past: wish + past perfect."},{s1:"The concert was so popular that there were no tickets left.",key:"SUCH",pre:"It was",post:"that there were no tickets left.",a:[["such a"],["popular concert"]],x:"such a + adjective + noun + that."}]},5:{title:"A month offline",intro:"an article in which a journalist describes a month without her smartphone",kind:"article",paras:["When my editor suggested that I spend a month without a smartphone, my first reaction was to laugh. I use my phone for everything: my alarm, my bank, my maps, my music and, if I'm honest, for filling every spare moment of the day. Within a minute of waking up I have usually checked three different apps. The idea of giving all that up felt less like a challenge and more like a punishment.","I agreed on one condition: I would be allowed an old-fashioned mobile that could only make calls and send texts. My family, I reasoned, needed to be able to reach me. What I hadn't anticipated was how complicated ordinary tasks would suddenly become. On the first morning I missed my usual train because I no longer had an app telling me it was running early, and I spent twenty minutes at the station studying a printed timetable as if it were an ancient text.","The first week was genuinely uncomfortable. I kept reaching into my pocket for a phone that wasn't there, a habit so automatic that I only noticed it when my hand came out empty. Waiting for anything – a bus, a coffee, a friend who was late – felt endless. I had forgotten, it seemed, how to simply stand somewhere and do nothing.","Somewhere in the second week, however, something shifted. Without the constant interruptions, I found I could read for an hour without looking up. I started noticing things on my walk to work: a café I had passed hundreds of times without seeing, the names of the streets. Conversations with friends became longer, partly because neither of us was glancing at a screen, and partly because I could no longer check a fact the moment an argument started.","Not everything improved. I got lost twice, missed an invitation that had been sent through a messaging app, and discovered that several of my friends found me far less convenient to deal with. ‘You're like a character in a historical drama,’ one of them complained, after I had phoned her – actually phoned her – to arrange a meeting.","When the month ended, I switched my smartphone back on and watched hundreds of notifications arrive. Almost none of them mattered. I haven't given the phone up, and I don't think I could. But it now lives in a drawer while I work, and I have deleted the two apps I used most. It turns out that the punishment I had dreaded was, in some ways, a holiday."],qs:[{q:"How did the writer feel when the idea was first suggested?",o:["keen to see how she would cope","unwilling to give up something she depended on","worried about what her family would think","confident that it would be easy"],a:1,x:"She uses the phone ‘for everything’ and giving it up ‘felt… like a punishment’."},{q:"Why does the writer mention the train timetable?",o:["to show she enjoyed rediscovering old skills","to give an example of an unexpected difficulty","to suggest that travel apps are unreliable","to explain why she was late for a meeting"],a:1,x:"‘What I hadn't anticipated was how complicated ordinary tasks would suddenly become.’"},{q:"What does the writer say about reaching for her phone in the first week?",o:["She did it without being aware of it.","She did it deliberately to test herself.","She stopped doing it after a few days.","She did it mostly while waiting for friends."],a:0,x:"‘a habit so automatic that I only noticed it when my hand came out empty’."},{q:"What change does the writer describe in paragraph 4?",o:["She started walking to work instead of taking the train.","She began to argue more with her friends.","She read the news more carefully.","She became more aware of her surroundings."],a:3,x:"She noticed ‘a café I had passed hundreds of times without seeing, the names of the streets’."},{q:"The friend's comment in paragraph 5 suggests that she",o:["admired the writer's determination.","wanted to try the experiment herself.","thought the writer was behaving in an old-fashioned way.","was upset that the writer had missed her invitation."],a:2,x:"‘like a character in a historical drama’ – phoning to arrange a meeting seemed old-fashioned."},{q:"What is the writer's attitude to her smartphone now?",o:["She uses it in a more limited way.","She has decided to stop using it completely.","She feels guilty about using it again.","She thinks the experiment was a waste of time."],a:0,x:"It ‘lives in a drawer while I work’ and she deleted the two apps she used most."}]},6:{title:"The shark scientist",intro:"an article about a marine biologist who studies sharks",paras:["As a child growing up in Lagos, Maya Okafor was terrified of the sea. {1} Today, she is one of the world's leading experts on sharks, and spends several months a year diving alongside them.","Her fear turned into fascination when she was fourteen and saw a documentary about whale sharks. {2} ‘I couldn't believe that something so enormous could be so gentle,’ she says. ‘I watched it again and again.’","After studying biology at university, she joined a research project in the Indian Ocean. The work was far from glamorous. {3} Most of her time was spent on a small boat, recording data and repairing equipment.","One of Okafor's main aims is to change the way people think about sharks. She points out that sharks kill fewer than ten people a year worldwide. {4} ‘We are a far bigger danger to them than they are to us,’ she says.","To reach a wider audience, she began posting short videos from her dives online. {5} Some of her clips have now been watched millions of times, and she regularly receives messages from children who say they want to become marine biologists.","Okafor admits that she still feels a moment of nervousness before every dive. {6} ‘Fear isn't something to be ashamed of,’ she says. ‘It just means you're paying attention.’"],options:["These huge fish, which can grow to over twelve metres long, feed only on tiny plankton.","But she sees this as a sign of respect for the animals rather than a weakness.","Her parents could never persuade her to go further than knee-deep into the water.","Since then, she has never been frightened of any animal in the ocean.","Humans, on the other hand, kill an estimated one hundred million sharks every year.","She had imagined days of diving with sharks, but the reality was very different.","At first, only a handful of friends and colleagues watched them."],key:[2,0,5,4,6,1],x:["C: ‘terrified of the sea’ is illustrated by never going ‘further than knee-deep’.","A: ‘These huge fish’ refers to the whale sharks; ‘so enormous… so gentle’ follows from ‘feed only on tiny plankton’.","F: ‘far from glamorous’ – she had imagined diving, but the reality was work on a boat.","E: ‘on the other hand’ contrasts sharks killing ten people with humans killing millions of sharks.","G: ‘At first’ a handful of people watched; later ‘millions of times’.","B: ‘this’ = her nervousness, which she sees as respect – leading to ‘Fear isn't something to be ashamed of’. D contradicts this paragraph."]},7:{title:"Starting young",intro:"an article in which four young people talk about starting their own businesses",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Lena, reusable cups",text:["I started selling reusable coffee cups made from recycled plastic when I was nineteen. The idea came from my job in a café, where I saw how many paper cups went in the bin every single day. My biggest mistake was ordering far too many in the first year – boxes of them filled my parents' garage for months. What I've learned is to grow slowly. These days I sell mainly to universities, and I've just taken on my first employee, which feels like a huge responsibility."]},{name:"Marcus, tutoring app",text:["My app connects school students with university students who can help them with maths. I built the first version myself, even though I'd only taught myself to code a few months earlier. The hardest thing was persuading parents to trust it – nobody wants to leave their child with a stranger online – so we introduced video checks for every tutor. I'm still at university full-time, so I work on the business late at night. My friends think I never sleep, and they're probably right."]},{name:"Aisha, vintage clothes",text:["I buy second-hand clothes at markets, repair them and sell them through my own website. It began as a hobby – I've always loved sewing – and I never imagined it would become a job. What surprised me was how much time goes on things nobody sees: taking photos, writing descriptions, answering emails. I don't earn a fortune, but I'm my own boss, and I love knowing that every item gets a second life instead of ending up in landfill."]},{name:"Josh, bike repairs",text:["I fix bikes in a small workshop behind my house. I left school at sixteen, and everyone – including my teachers – told me I was making a big mistake. To be honest, there were times in the first year when I thought they were right. Word of mouth has been everything; I've never paid a penny for advertising. The part I enjoy most is showing customers how to do simple repairs themselves. Some people say that's bad for business, but they always come back for the bigger jobs."]}],qs:[{q:"was advised by others not to follow their chosen path?",a:3,x:"Josh: everyone ‘told me I was making a big mistake’."},{q:"learned a new skill shortly before starting the business?",a:1,x:"Marcus had ‘only taught myself to code a few months earlier’."},{q:"made an error by buying too much stock?",a:0,x:"Lena: ‘ordering far too many in the first year’."},{q:"had to find a way to make customers feel safe?",a:1,x:"Marcus introduced ‘video checks for every tutor’ so parents would trust the app."},{q:"did not originally intend to earn money from their interest?",a:2,x:"Aisha: ‘It began as a hobby… I never imagined it would become a job.’"},{q:"gets new customers without spending money on promotion?",a:3,x:"Josh: ‘Word of mouth… I've never paid a penny for advertising.’"},{q:"got the idea from something they noticed at work?",a:0,x:"Lena saw paper cups going in the bin at the café where she worked."},{q:"mentions tasks that take up more time than expected?",a:2,x:"Aisha: ‘how much time goes on things nobody sees’."},{q:"runs the business alongside their studies?",a:1,x:"Marcus is ‘still at university full-time’."},{q:"shares knowledge in a way some think could reduce income?",a:3,x:"Josh teaches customers simple repairs – ‘Some people say that's bad for business’."}]}}}),LZ.data.tests.push({id:"t8",title:"Test 8",topics:"Board game cafés · Why cats purr · Volunteering abroad · The audition · The town without cars · Languages later in life",parts:{1:{title:"Back to the table",text:`Board game cafés, where customers pay a small fee to play games while they eat and drink, have {0} up in cities all over the world. Their success has taken many people by {1}, in an age when so much of our entertainment {2} place on screens.

Owners say the appeal {3} in the social side of playing. Unlike video games, board games bring people together around a table and {4} them to talk to each other. Many cafés employ staff whose job is to recommend games and explain the rules, so that beginners don't feel {5} out.

Some groups of friends have {6} a habit of meeting every Friday evening, and a few cafés even {7} tournaments with prizes. For many people, it seems, there is still no {8} for sitting down face to face with friends.`,example:{o:["sprung","jumped","leapt","grown"],a:0},gaps:[{o:["shock","wonder","surprise","amazement"],a:2,x:"Fixed phrase: take somebody by surprise."},{o:["takes","gives","has","makes"],a:0,x:"take place = happen."},{o:["stays","lies","sits","stands"],a:1,x:"The appeal / answer / problem lies in something."},{o:["make","let","have","encourage"],a:3,x:"encourage somebody to do something. Make, let and have are followed by the infinitive without ‘to’."},{o:["left","kept","shut","put"],a:0,x:"feel left out = feel excluded from a group."},{o:["done","made","got","taken"],a:1,x:"make a habit of doing something."},{o:["keep","carry","bear","hold"],a:3,x:"hold a tournament / competition / meeting = organise and have it."},{o:["exchange","substitute","alternative","change"],a:1,x:"there is no substitute for something. ‘Alternative’ is followed by ‘to’."}]},2:{title:"Why do cats purr?",text:`Most people assume that cats purr {0} they are happy. While this is often true, scientists have discovered that the story is rather more complicated {1} that. Cats also purr when they are frightened, injured or even giving birth, {2} suggests that purring may help them to calm down. There is also evidence that the vibrations could help injuries heal {3} quickly.

Exactly {4} cats produce the sound was a mystery for a long time. We now know that it is made by muscles in the throat, and that it continues {5} the cat is breathing in or out.

Interestingly, not all members of the cat family can purr. Lions and tigers can roar, but they are unable {6} purr in the same way. Domestic cats, {7} the other hand, purr throughout their lives. So next time your cat is purring on your lap, it may be happy – or it may simply be looking {8} itself.`,example:{a:"because"},gaps:[{a:["than"],x:"Comparative: more complicated than that."},{a:["which"],x:"‘which’ refers to the whole previous idea."},{a:["more"],x:"Comparative adverb: heal more quickly."},{a:["how"],x:"Exactly how cats produce the sound = the way they produce it."},{a:["whether"],x:"whether … or: it continues in both cases."},{a:["to"],x:"be unable to do something."},{a:["on"],x:"on the other hand."},{a:["after"],x:"look after yourself = take care of yourself."}]},3:{title:"A year to make a difference",text:`Volunteering abroad has become {0} popular with school leavers.
Many say the experience helps them develop {1}
and that it can be {2} when they later apply for jobs.
However, critics argue that some programmes are poorly {3}.

Volunteers without the right {4} may do more harm than good,
especially in {5} such as teaching or building work.
It is also {6} to check how much of the fee reaches local communities.
The most {7} volunteers are those who stay long enough
to build real {8} with the people they work alongside.`,example:{w:"EXTREME",a:"extremely"},gaps:[{w:"INDEPENDENT",a:["independence"],x:"Noun after ‘develop’: develop independence."},{w:"BENEFIT",a:["beneficial"],x:"Adjective after ‘be’: beneficial = helpful."},{w:"ORGANISE",a:["organised","organized"],x:"Past participle after ‘poorly’: poorly organised."},{w:"QUALIFY",a:["qualifications"],x:"Plural noun after ‘the right’: qualifications."},{w:"ACT",a:["activities"],x:"Plural noun: activities such as teaching."},{w:"ADVISE",a:["advisable"],x:"Adjective: it is advisable to = it is a good idea to."},{w:"SUCCESS",a:["successful"],x:"Adjective after ‘the most’: successful."},{w:"RELATION",a:["relationships"],x:"build relationships with people."}]},4:{items:[{s1:"I'd prefer to stay at home tonight.",key:"RATHER",pre:"I",post:"at home tonight.",a:[["would rather"],["stay"]],x:"would rather + infinitive without ‘to’."},{s1:"They cancelled the match because of the rain.",key:"CALLED",pre:"The match",post:"because of the rain.",a:[["was called"],["off"]],x:"Passive of the phrasal verb call off = cancel."},{s1:"It's possible that Sam forgot about the meeting.",key:"MIGHT",pre:"Sam",post:"about the meeting.",a:[["might have"],["forgotten"]],x:"Possibility in the past: might have + past participle."},{s1:"Nobody in the class is taller than Ben.",key:"TALLEST",pre:"Ben",post:"in the class.",a:[["is the"],["tallest"]],x:"nobody is taller than = the tallest."},{s1:"I started learning Spanish three years ago.",key:"BEEN",pre:"I",post:"Spanish for three years.",a:[["have been"],["learning"]],x:"Present perfect continuous + for: have been learning."},{s1:"‘I'm sorry I broke your glasses,’ Emma said to me.",key:"APOLOGISED",pre:"Emma",post:"my glasses.",a:[["apologised for"],["breaking","having broken"]],x:"apologise for + -ing."}]},5:{title:"The audition",intro:"an extract from a novel",kind:"extract",paras:["The waiting room was painted a colour that someone had probably described as ‘calming’. It wasn't working. Eight of us sat along one wall, each holding an instrument case as if it might escape, and nobody spoke. I had been told to arrive at ten. It was now twenty to eleven, and every time the door opened my stomach turned over.","My mother had wanted to come in with me, but I had made her wait in the car park. At the time, this had seemed like a sign of maturity. Now, looking at the girl opposite me, whose father was quietly going through her music with her, I rather regretted it. She looked about twelve and was completely calm, which I found deeply annoying.","When my name was finally called, I walked into a room much smaller than I had imagined. Three people sat behind a table. The woman in the middle smiled and asked me what I was going to play, although it was written on the sheet in front of her. I realised later that she was simply giving me the chance to hear my own voice before I had to do anything more difficult.","I had practised the piece so many times that my fingers knew it better than I did. For the first minute, that was exactly the problem: I played it perfectly and felt nothing, like someone reading a shopping list aloud. Then, in the slow middle section, I made a mistake – a small one, a wrong note that nobody but a musician would have noticed. And strangely, it woke me up. I stopped trying to be perfect and started, for the first time that morning, to listen.","When I finished, there was a silence that seemed to last for a very long time. Then the man on the left asked me why I had chosen that particular piece. I told him the truth, which was that my grandfather used to play it at family parties, badly, and always stopped before the end. ‘I wanted to find out how it finished,’ I said. He wrote something down and, for the first time, looked up and smiled.","Outside, my mother asked how it had gone, and I said I didn't know, which was true. I had made a mistake in front of three of the most important people in my musical life. But as we drove home, I found myself humming the slow section, and it seemed to me that I had played it better with the wrong note than I ever had without it."],qs:[{q:"How did the writer feel in the waiting room?",o:["bored by the long delay","anxious about what was going to happen","relaxed by the calm atmosphere","curious about the other candidates"],a:1,x:"‘every time the door opened my stomach turned over’ – the calming colour ‘wasn't working’."},{q:"Why did the writer regret making her mother wait outside?",o:["She saw another candidate being helped by a parent.","She needed help carrying her instrument.","Her mother had been upset by the decision.","She had left her music in the car."],a:0,x:"The girl opposite had a father ‘quietly going through her music with her’."},{q:"What does the writer suggest about the woman's first question?",o:["It showed she hadn't read the information.","It was meant to test the candidate's memory.","It was a kind way of helping her settle.","It was the same question everyone was asked."],a:2,x:"‘she was simply giving me the chance to hear my own voice before I had to do anything more difficult’."},{q:"What effect did the mistake have on the writer?",o:["It made her lose confidence.","It made her play more carelessly.","It made the examiners stop her.","It helped her engage properly with the music."],a:3,x:"‘it woke me up. I stopped trying to be perfect and started… to listen.’"},{q:"Why did the man smile at the end of paragraph 5?",o:["He found the story about the grandfather funny.","He was impressed by the reason for her choice.","He was relieved that the audition was over.","He wanted to hide his disappointment."],a:1,x:"He smiles after hearing her personal reason and writes something down – a positive reaction."},{q:"At the end of the extract, the writer feels that",o:["her performance was better than she first thought.","she should practise more before another audition.","her mother had been right to want to come in.","she will never forget the embarrassment of her mistake."],a:0,x:"‘I had played it better with the wrong note than I ever had without it.’"}]},6:{title:"The town that gave its streets back",intro:"an article about a small town in northern Spain that removed cars from its centre",paras:["Twenty years ago, the centre of the town was full of traffic. Cars filled the narrow streets and parked on the pavements, and the air was thick with fumes. {1} Today, those same streets are quiet enough to hear birds singing, and children walk to school on their own.","The change began when the new mayor announced that cars would no longer be allowed in the historic centre. {2} Shop owners were particularly worried, convinced that their customers would simply go elsewhere.","In fact, the opposite happened. {3} Small businesses that had been struggling started to attract new customers, and several empty shops reopened.","The town did not ban cars completely. Delivery vans can still enter at certain times, and residents are allowed to drive to their own garages. {4} As a result, drivers no longer circle the streets looking for somewhere to stop.","Perhaps the most striking result has been in road safety. {5} Visitors often notice how elderly residents cross the road slowly, without looking nervously over their shoulders.","Other cities have sent planners to study the town's approach. Its supporters admit that what works in a small town cannot simply be copied in a capital city. {6} ‘You don't have to change everything at once,’ says one planner. ‘Start with one street and let people see the difference.’"],options:["Once people could walk around comfortably, they began to spend more time in the centre.","The mayor was forced to abandon the plan after only a few months.","However, parking on the street is not permitted anywhere in the centre.","Many families had moved out to the suburbs because it no longer felt like a pleasant place to live.","But they believe that the basic lesson applies everywhere.","Not everyone welcomed the idea.","For more than a decade, not a single person has been killed in a traffic accident in the town."],key:[3,5,0,2,6,4],x:["D: families moving out follows the description of traffic and fumes, and contrasts with ‘Today…’.","F: ‘Not everyone welcomed the idea’ introduces the worried shop owners.","A: ‘the opposite happened’ – instead of losing customers, people spent more time in the centre.","C: ‘However’ adds a limit to what residents can do; no street parking explains why drivers no longer circle.","G: gives the ‘striking result’ in road safety. B is wrong – the plan clearly continued.","E: ‘But’ contrasts with ‘cannot simply be copied’ and leads to the planner's advice."]},7:{title:"Never too late",intro:"an article in which four adults talk about learning a new language",who:"people",stem:"Which person…",sections:[{name:"Tomasz, Japanese",text:["I started learning Japanese at forty-five, after a work trip to Osaka where I was embarrassed that I couldn't even say ‘thank you’ properly. Writing has been the biggest challenge – there are three different systems, and I still need a dictionary for most signs. What keeps me going is a weekly video call with a retired teacher in Kyoto. We've never met in person, but she's become a real friend, and she corrects me without ever making me feel stupid."]},{name:"Rachel, Portuguese",text:["I moved to Lisbon for my husband's job and assumed I'd pick up Portuguese naturally. I was wrong. People switched to English as soon as they heard my accent, so after a year I could barely order a coffee. In the end I signed up for an intensive course – four hours a day for a month. It was exhausting, but it worked. Now I ask people to keep speaking Portuguese to me, even when I struggle, and most of them are delighted to."]},{name:"Omar, sign language",text:["I learned British Sign Language because my nephew was born deaf, and I wanted to be able to talk to him properly. People imagine it's just a set of gestures, but it has its own grammar, and facial expressions are an essential part of the meaning. My nephew is ten now and signs far faster than I do – he laughs at my mistakes constantly. Volunteering at a local deaf club has improved my signing more than any class."]},{name:"Helen, Italian",text:["Italian was something I'd always meant to learn, and when I retired I finally had the time. I use an app every morning, but on its own I don't think it would have been enough. The real turning point was a two-week homestay in Bologna with a family who spoke no English at all. Honestly, the first few days were frightening. By the end, though, I was dreaming in Italian. I'm going back next spring, and this time I'll stay for a month."]}],qs:[{q:"wanted to be able to communicate with a relative?",a:2,x:"Omar learned sign language for his deaf nephew."},{q:"found that local people's behaviour slowed their progress?",a:1,x:"Rachel: people ‘switched to English as soon as they heard my accent’."},{q:"felt ashamed of not knowing something very basic?",a:0,x:"Tomasz was ‘embarrassed that I couldn't even say thank you properly’."},{q:"says one feature of the language is often misunderstood?",a:2,x:"Omar: ‘People imagine it's just a set of gestures, but it has its own grammar.’"},{q:"found being surrounded by the language difficult at first?",a:3,x:"Helen: ‘the first few days were frightening’ in a family that spoke no English."},{q:"has a teacher who is patient about their mistakes?",a:0,x:"Tomasz's teacher ‘corrects me without ever making me feel stupid’."},{q:"chose a way of learning that was very tiring?",a:1,x:"Rachel's intensive course ‘was exhausting, but it worked’."},{q:"is planning a longer stay abroad?",a:3,x:"Helen: ‘this time I'll stay for a month’."},{q:"is often teased by someone younger who is more skilled?",a:2,x:"Omar's nephew ‘signs far faster than I do – he laughs at my mistakes constantly’."},{q:"thinks a digital tool alone would not have been enough?",a:3,x:"Helen uses an app, ‘but on its own I don't think it would have been enough’."}]}}});
